WEBVTT

03:27.621 --> 03:30.890
All right, we're going to go ahead and get started. October 20th,

03:31.483 --> 03:35.152
it is 4:01 PM, and we're going to have our start of work session.

03:35.633 --> 03:39.367
Did want to excuse some of our staff is out of town, and I

03:39.495 --> 03:42.892
do know that our city manager Ben is online participating as well as others,

03:42.908 --> 03:46.674
I'm sure. Working out of town. Yeah, and they're working,

03:46.946 --> 03:50.654
they're at the ICMA conference and working in Southern

03:50.703 --> 03:54.271
California. So we appreciate them joining us online.

03:54.482 --> 03:58.613
And we'll go ahead and get started. This first item is

04:00.239 --> 04:04.248
the discussion about the MAP funding capacity and the tier prioritization.

04:04.720 --> 04:07.885
And I believe that's Kyra. Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

04:18.039 --> 04:21.797
About a little bit of time to talk about the

04:22.297 --> 04:23.958
current status of the MAP program.

04:31.287 --> 04:34.546
Sweet. Okay, so, uh, main things

04:34.562 --> 04:37.662
that we want to talk about today are just our funding capacity and the tier

04:37.727 --> 04:40.940
prioritization. Just as a reminder,

04:40.957 --> 04:44.448
I just provided a report to you guys going over how

04:44.480 --> 04:48.066
we closed out this last fiscal year. So we had

04:49.181 --> 04:52.460
a huge jump from the beginning to the end of the year. We increased by

04:52.960 --> 04:56.074
about 139% in applications.

04:56.990 --> 05:00.685
So while we had 281 over the full year, 200 of

05:01.185 --> 05:03.862
those were just from the second part of the year. So the demand is there.

05:04.234 --> 05:08.006
We're definitely serving the community. And some of

05:08.038 --> 05:11.890
those discrepancies in the approvals and closings were just that some of those closed

05:12.079 --> 05:13.427
simply after the fiscal year.

05:15.626 --> 05:19.387
So, um, demand right now just for this current year has

05:19.565 --> 05:23.020
been increased again from the last year, but as you can see, we can only

05:23.552 --> 05:27.177
fund about 1 to 3 grants per month. That's obviously going

05:27.677 --> 05:29.545
to depend on their tier status and how much we're funding,

05:30.624 --> 05:34.615
but you can see that we're getting many more applications than just the 1

05:34.808 --> 05:38.457
or 3 that we can fund. And so what we're doing right

05:38.505 --> 05:41.768
now is we're rolling over the additional grants to still give folks the opportunity

05:41.784 --> 05:45.855
if funds Opened up, and they

05:46.355 --> 05:49.493
can only roll over for 3 months. Most of those guys are all of

05:49.993 --> 05:53.340
our Tier 3s, because obviously the general applicants are taking priority

05:53.437 --> 05:55.840
after our Tier 1s and 2s. And so,

05:57.789 --> 06:01.960
yes. This is just an overview of

06:01.992 --> 06:05.904
kind of how the grants are being disbursed. So about half of them are going

06:05.952 --> 06:08.610
to Tier 3s, the general public.

06:09.760 --> 06:13.287
The other half is going between Tier 1s and 2s.

06:15.775 --> 06:18.990
Of course, we're prioritizing AMI when we

06:19.490 --> 06:22.189
start to get a lot of applicants, just whoever has the biggest need and the

06:22.689 --> 06:26.129
lowest AMI. Quick question. Remind me which tier military

06:26.226 --> 06:28.040
falls into. They're Tier 2.

06:32.120 --> 06:34.925
So is it— can I ask a question? Yeah, please.

06:34.974 --> 06:38.266
Kiara, my question is really regarding the Tier 1

06:38.315 --> 06:42.263
and Tier 2. Are we Are there Tier 1 and Tier 2 applications

06:42.763 --> 06:45.343
that we're receiving that we're unable to process? Absolutely not.

06:49.191 --> 06:52.469
Okay. So the question today is just to kind of discuss

06:52.742 --> 06:55.698
if Tier 3s continue to represent that half mark,

06:56.437 --> 07:00.191
they are getting a large amount of the fund, do we want to continue

07:00.224 --> 07:03.320
the approach of rolling folks over and allowing them to continue,

07:05.192 --> 07:08.576
or looking at capacity a little bit different? One item for

07:09.076 --> 07:12.653
discussion would be prioritization. prioritizing within our

07:13.153 --> 07:16.371
Tier 3 applicants. And so looking at ways that maybe

07:16.871 --> 07:20.639
we are, you know, putting our skilled trade workers or however we

07:21.139 --> 07:24.072
want to determine those folks, do we want to start to do anything in that

07:24.120 --> 07:28.033
nature? Or do— like, yeah, how do you guys want to— with

07:28.066 --> 07:30.449
the overflow and limited funds?

07:33.524 --> 07:35.829
And sorry, we'll answer those in a second.

07:36.409 --> 07:39.535
Broader RDA discussions, which we've kind of talked about in past

07:39.567 --> 07:41.485
conversations, other ways that we can use RDA funds.

07:43.502 --> 07:46.449
did talk about a home revitalization program,

07:47.206 --> 07:50.958
which, uh, depending on how we wanted to use RDA funds,

07:50.991 --> 07:54.910
we could split it with other funding mechanisms. The major thing

07:55.088 --> 07:58.672
would be a staffing capacity for, for that kind

07:59.172 --> 08:00.174
of a project to be pushed forward.

08:02.577 --> 08:05.552
Um, and just to close, reiterating, um,

08:05.907 --> 08:09.916
that we're just looking to discuss how we want to prioritize

08:10.191 --> 08:11.646
with the limited funds that we have. Thank you.

08:13.698 --> 08:16.582
Can I ask you a— oh, go ahead,

08:16.663 --> 08:20.027
Melissa. No, you're— go for it. I'll wait until you're done. So within

08:20.123 --> 08:23.216
Tier 3, how are they currently? Is it first come, first serve?

08:23.985 --> 08:27.061
It's not ranked by AMI? It's first come, first serve, and then by AMI.

08:27.558 --> 08:30.683
Okay. So if they're putting— I mean, obviously, if— because then now if we have

08:30.699 --> 08:34.111
2 AMIs, whoever got their application in. If they have similar AMI,

08:34.304 --> 08:37.556
whoever's application came in first. That to me seems

08:37.925 --> 08:41.903
okay. I don't know if we need to— further delineate based on job

08:41.919 --> 08:45.291
class, or sometimes government programs can just get more and

08:45.791 --> 08:49.498
more complicated. Absolutely. So if it's working well the way it is, I'm fine

08:49.546 --> 08:53.689
if we continue to roll over and first come, first serve.

08:54.524 --> 08:58.490
It's an awesome program and obviously really popular. So that

08:59.020 --> 09:03.146
just— those are my thoughts. I tend to agree with Councilmember Hewish

09:03.258 --> 09:06.956
on that. I'm also wondering, you mentioned

09:07.314 --> 09:10.288
they can only roll over for 3 months. Is that just part of our policy?

09:10.790 --> 09:14.883
Mm-hmm. Could we adjust that to make it a longer one and then eliminate

09:15.093 --> 09:18.364
some of that issue? Because I don't want people to miss out just because

09:18.477 --> 09:22.800
it's popular. And I already answered

09:23.300 --> 09:26.600
my thoughts about whether or not we should tier Tier 3. One other

09:27.100 --> 09:29.950
thing that we've kind of tossed around, and I don't know if it's ever been

09:30.450 --> 09:33.955
while you're here, but would be to do some sort of ADU grant

09:34.227 --> 09:37.774
so that existing homeowners that have the space

09:37.886 --> 09:41.673
to convert into an ADU and then find

09:42.173 --> 09:45.926
a way to make whatever the rent they charge for that or something within our

09:46.426 --> 09:49.649
parameters so it kind of fits that affordable housing thing. I think that

09:50.323 --> 09:55.201
is another piece of what we should be looking at. And I know that funds

09:55.474 --> 09:59.084
are limited now and they'll always have a limit. But have we

09:59.148 --> 10:03.318
done any projections of what we think with the next

10:03.818 --> 10:07.393
few bigger projects that are coming online and when they hit property

10:07.409 --> 10:10.713
tax next year, what that's going to look like for our

10:11.213 --> 10:15.028
fund? Not that far into depth, but I do have something that I'd

10:15.528 --> 10:18.091
love to chat with you guys and present kind of where I'm at. I'm at

10:18.591 --> 10:21.620
a good holding point where we could go to a couple different directions. And so

10:22.887 --> 10:25.887
that would discuss home revitalization and of course ADU.

10:27.137 --> 10:30.295
Mostly staffing and funding as a mechanism is what we just

10:30.795 --> 10:33.934
need to kind of nail down. Could we— Mayor, sorry, Mayor,

10:33.966 --> 10:37.461
could we ask staff to kind of look at the projections

10:37.557 --> 10:41.420
of what our affordable housing will be? I mean, we have some idea because

10:41.920 --> 10:45.155
we're starting to see what things are worth property-wise,

10:46.069 --> 10:49.483
and with us knowing that there's a specific calculation for that,

10:49.916 --> 10:52.641
I'd like to have a better idea 1, 2 years out.

10:53.122 --> 10:56.971
Because that will help me as a decision maker prioritize what's

10:57.471 --> 11:00.483
the next thing that we try to accomplish with this. I think, and I'll jump

11:00.983 --> 11:05.038
in because this might change some of the comments, and I do believe Kimberly's online

11:05.134 --> 11:08.742
as well. So correct me if I'm wrong because I'm going off

11:08.758 --> 11:12.623
memory here. But I think one of the questions for consideration

11:13.123 --> 11:16.232
here should be, do we want to fund this more?

11:16.633 --> 11:20.027
So when we're talking about, you know, We can only do 1 or 2 a

11:20.527 --> 11:24.208
month, and the popularity of the project,

11:24.369 --> 11:27.324
it's very popular. And now that the word is out, and I'm going to tell

11:27.824 --> 11:31.056
you the word is out. Mm-hmm. Specifically like UFA, there's a ton

11:31.556 --> 11:35.605
of those guys that have been following this, and they're now seeing

11:35.718 --> 11:39.981
what it can do for them. And that's one of our own internal organizations,

11:39.997 --> 11:43.119
and they've all been applying for it that I've talked

11:43.619 --> 11:46.863
to. To me, you know, it would help

11:47.363 --> 11:50.168
that prioritization if we just fund the program a little bit more. And we've got

11:50.668 --> 11:54.308
dollars sitting there that are restricted. We don't have any other ideas,

11:54.404 --> 11:58.223
so do we just fund it more? But I think that's the reason for Councilmember

11:58.271 --> 12:01.577
Clark's question. I think we just need to know budget projections,

12:01.625 --> 12:05.701
right? What's in there? What has been allocated? What's still remaining in

12:05.797 --> 12:09.022
the fund? And then recognizing—

12:09.522 --> 12:13.402
I have those numbers right now. We were allocated $1.2 Um, at the beginning

12:13.434 --> 12:16.431
of the fiscal year, we've used about $350K.

12:17.185 --> 12:20.648
Um, so we've got roughly $840K left

12:21.148 --> 12:25.217
over. Doing nothing there. So there's still $840K in there. So have

12:25.717 --> 12:29.673
we projected out? Because we've already talked about using some of these RDA dollars

12:29.785 --> 12:33.248
to fund water for the affordable housing that's coming in Scarlet Ridge.

12:33.296 --> 12:37.240
Has that been deducted from the fund? No. And, and those— and that's

12:37.740 --> 12:41.395
kind of what would need to be considered for us to kind of maneuver

12:41.491 --> 12:45.181
with this. I mean, I 100% agree with Councilmember Clark. I think we just need

12:45.681 --> 12:49.304
to see those projections so that we understand what's coming, what was left

12:49.336 --> 12:52.384
in reserve currently. It sounds like there's $800 and change,

12:53.026 --> 12:56.780
and what we've committed, because we have committed that to the water resourcing,

12:57.374 --> 13:00.695
obviously. Well, if we continue on at the rate that

13:00.727 --> 13:03.711
we're going now, in just the first 3 months we've distributed $350,000.

13:04.932 --> 13:08.032
So just, I mean, from there, if we did that every 3

13:08.048 --> 13:11.486
months, we would exhaust the fund for sure. And so is that something

13:11.502 --> 13:13.526
that we want to do? Do we just want to go ahead and have a

13:13.574 --> 13:17.140
clean wash of the funds, or do we want to help more folks,

13:17.205 --> 13:20.466
or do we— You're talking about the $1.2 million? I'm confused what the ask is.

13:20.835 --> 13:24.787
So again, how you're saying if we wanted a projection to see

13:24.835 --> 13:27.470
how the rest of the year is going to play out, we can tell by

13:27.970 --> 13:31.518
the first 3 months spending $350K that we're going to exhaust

13:32.018 --> 13:35.274
the fund. But that's also during— I'm sorry, that's the $1.2 million.

13:35.595 --> 13:38.838
That's what I'm hearing you say, right? And that's what we've already allocated.

13:39.063 --> 13:42.756
So who cares if we exhaust it? I thought that was the idea for sure.

13:43.799 --> 13:46.833
So I mean, that, that's where I would land on that. But what I do

13:47.333 --> 13:51.007
want to know is what's still in reserve and what's coming next year so that

13:51.507 --> 13:54.828
we can do some, some planning for fiscal 2027,

13:55.021 --> 13:58.472
fiscal 2028. In my mind, that's what

13:58.972 --> 14:02.887
I would like to see before— even before we start to add ADU

14:03.387 --> 14:07.077
or some other equation or some other thing to the mix.

14:07.864 --> 14:11.749
And then I'd like really to have a discussion about how successful this program

14:12.022 --> 14:15.458
is and, and what are the benefits that it has been bringing

14:15.714 --> 14:19.038
to those families. In fact, it would be great to hear some testimonies—

14:19.503 --> 14:22.955
testimonials of some who might be willing to do that. And my last

14:23.455 --> 14:27.446
thought, Kiara, for you is I don't know that I 100%

14:27.946 --> 14:31.582
agree with what Councilmember Hewish said. I mean, I agree with him that it's functioning.

14:32.095 --> 14:36.985
My concern is if those Tier 3 applicants

14:37.065 --> 14:40.897
are all being evaluated on what the AMI is, then those at 120

14:41.397 --> 14:44.023
or 110 or 100 are pretty much out of the running right from the get-go,

14:44.039 --> 14:47.213
right? And I don't know that that's fair to make them jump through those

14:47.245 --> 14:51.302
hoops because what's the prognosis or probability of any of them receiving

14:51.334 --> 14:54.994
the benefit. So my thought process is

14:55.090 --> 14:57.466
I think we should institute a lottery for those Tier 3s.

14:58.557 --> 15:02.249
And what I would consider doing is adding— so if you're a Tier

15:02.749 --> 15:05.428
3 and you're 120 AMI, you get one,

15:05.861 --> 15:09.152
you get your name in the hat once. If you're 110, you get

15:09.652 --> 15:13.245
1.5. If you're a Tier 3

15:13.745 --> 15:16.584
and you have 100 AMI, you get 2, then 2.5 for 90,

15:16.841 --> 15:20.408
and then 3 for 80, and then Let the lottery decide who gets

15:20.908 --> 15:23.981
those pieces. To me, that seems fairer, that those Tier

15:24.481 --> 15:27.796
3s, then everyone has not an equal chance,

15:27.845 --> 15:31.507
but a chance of drawing that

15:32.007 --> 15:35.894
application. A fair chance. A fair chance. So another

15:36.394 --> 15:39.872
question too. On most of these ones that have been approved, are they

15:40.372 --> 15:43.639
getting approved at the full amount, or at 80%, or at 70%?

15:43.995 --> 15:47.106
Because they could, We could approve some, but like,

15:47.154 --> 15:50.813
what's the average been? So for Tier 1s,

15:51.506 --> 15:54.395
Eagle Mountain City, they're all getting the full 25 typically.

15:55.301 --> 15:57.823
Then it starts to depend on the price of the home if they're gonna get

15:58.323 --> 16:02.156
the full 225 or 7.5%

16:02.480 --> 16:05.985
or 3.5%, whichever one's higher or

16:06.195 --> 16:09.652
lower. Sorry, I really like the idea of doing some

16:10.152 --> 16:13.445
sort of lottery. I'm not a fan of tiering

16:13.493 --> 16:16.667
it based on, like, your profession and stuff like that.

16:16.763 --> 16:20.691
We saw some of that with, like, essential workers and whatnot. And anyway,

16:20.995 --> 16:24.586
I— It's arbitrary. Yeah, it's— I think it makes sense to

16:25.260 --> 16:29.139
tier it with the partnering entities and ones that are a part of the RDA.

16:29.347 --> 16:32.794
But for other things, I'm not on board with that.

16:33.259 --> 16:36.513
I guess, too, us having an idea is going to tell us whether or not

16:36.882 --> 16:40.439
we should look at revitalization grants, because if we're exhausting all the

16:40.487 --> 16:43.725
funds with the mortgage assistance program, then we shouldn't be going there.

16:44.093 --> 16:47.266
Another question that kind of came up as we were talking is,

16:48.420 --> 16:51.897
and this is probably a legal or a Ben question,

16:52.186 --> 16:55.327
but if there was an entity that wanted to donate

16:55.827 --> 16:58.996
money to our affordable housing fund, could it be set

16:59.496 --> 17:03.692
up in such a way that they could add to what

17:03.788 --> 17:07.441
we're able to get for mortgage assistance and

17:08.034 --> 17:11.911
revitalization grants? Like, could we start accepting donations

17:12.411 --> 17:15.292
or contributions if it's earmarked very specifically? Marcus,

17:15.965 --> 17:19.795
looking at you. I mean, the, the answer is the normal answer. It depends.

17:19.907 --> 17:23.656
But generally speaking, yes, that is possible. See, and I think

17:23.672 --> 17:27.598
that one, I want us to give out as much help as possible

17:28.098 --> 17:31.286
because I don't want the money just sitting there doing nothing. I don't really want

17:31.786 --> 17:35.108
us to throttle back and say, well, we had 6 in

17:35.220 --> 17:38.319
June, so that means we can only have 2 in— you know what I mean?

17:38.415 --> 17:42.189
Like, I want us to like go all out because that fund's gonna continue

17:42.689 --> 17:46.444
to grow. So I would hope that you can come back

17:46.944 --> 17:49.976
and give us numbers of projections of where things are headed with that.

17:50.377 --> 17:53.203
And then I think we'll be ready to make decisions about what we need to

17:53.703 --> 17:53.862
go with next.

17:59.050 --> 18:02.232
So when this, when this program was created, I stated

18:02.264 --> 18:05.381
that this would become the most successful program in the entire state,

18:05.895 --> 18:09.494
and it has been. And it's, it's because it was set up under the right

18:09.994 --> 18:13.141
principles and with the right format, and it's great, and it's awesome to see that

18:13.641 --> 18:17.913
this is going on. I can, I can get behind either AMI

18:18.668 --> 18:21.737
or a lottery as long as it is weighted toward the lower AMI. Thank you.

18:22.188 --> 18:25.276
I could get behind either of those. I'm fine with that for Tier 3.

18:26.148 --> 18:29.830
And I don't want to

18:29.943 --> 18:32.824
bring employment into Tier 3. That's just mine.

18:33.487 --> 18:36.966
I prefer the money to go to MAP as opposed to an ADU type

18:37.466 --> 18:39.853
situation just because, I mean, if we did the ADU situation,

18:41.848 --> 18:44.382
I would only feel comfortable if it goes to people who are in need.

18:44.446 --> 18:47.981
Like they, I don't know, maybe seniors or that have limited

18:48.481 --> 18:51.349
assets to otherwise create an ADU or something. I think it just gets, It would

18:51.397 --> 18:54.389
be a longer conversation to see if we could come up with the right ingredients

18:54.889 --> 18:58.966
to where I'd feel good about that. MAP is a slam dunk to continue supporting

18:58.998 --> 19:02.220
that. Eagle Mountain has a lot of properties on the market right now.

19:03.000 --> 19:04.071
This helps solve some of that.

19:10.613 --> 19:13.694
Okay. Okay. Thank you, guys. If there's nothing else, we'll move along.

19:13.759 --> 19:17.244
Thanks for that information. And you got what you need, right? Yep.

19:17.390 --> 19:20.934
Thank you. Thank you. The next item up, 1B,

19:21.030 --> 19:23.854
is Alternative Energy Overlay Zone Revisions.

19:24.544 --> 19:27.576
Perfect timing, Mr. Evan. I know this,

19:28.074 --> 19:30.191
we worked on this a little while ago, but there were a few things that

19:30.691 --> 19:33.994
we wanted to tweak. So Evan heard those items

19:34.090 --> 19:37.876
and he's coming back to share with us some

19:37.941 --> 19:41.326
potential changes, or amendments, I'll call them.

19:53.747 --> 19:57.697
Yes, so Mayor and Council, these suggested

19:58.197 --> 20:02.405
changes were all based on the feedback that you provided when this overlay

20:02.905 --> 20:06.133
zone was approved. And so I tried to tackle every single

20:06.633 --> 20:09.896
one of them that I could. We'll just walk through

20:10.396 --> 20:14.167
these really quick for you. So the first one,

20:14.652 --> 20:19.069
we modified some definitions in here. One of the requests was to disassociate

20:19.569 --> 20:22.678
the energy overlay zone from the RTI overlay zone, which is under the

20:23.178 --> 20:25.995
purpose section at the beginning. So that language was just removed.

20:27.783 --> 20:31.393
And then we took a different approach. There was some debate back and

20:31.409 --> 20:35.036
forth, if you recall, about how to approach nuclear technologies,

20:35.230 --> 20:38.472
whether or not we want to specify which ones we want or

20:39.243 --> 20:41.510
prevent some of the ones we don't want or anything like that.

20:42.603 --> 20:46.455
And so it kind of took a bit of a middle-of-the-road approach where essentially

20:46.632 --> 20:49.924
we're listing a set of

20:49.940 --> 20:53.167
technologies that we prefer, but then

20:53.667 --> 20:56.956
leaving the door open to say if there are other technologies that, you know,

20:57.197 --> 21:00.723
a company would reasonably believe that we would potentially accept, they may bring those

21:00.997 --> 21:03.955
for City Council to review at that time.

21:06.110 --> 21:09.676
Obviously, there's certain points in the language or different requirements that are going to

21:10.176 --> 21:13.371
kind of eliminate some of the technologies

21:13.435 --> 21:16.916
anyway based on water requirements or based on certain safety requirements

21:17.416 --> 21:21.147
and things like that. It kind of, you know, it eliminates some

21:21.647 --> 21:25.251
things automatically. But generally speaking, we're now indicating

21:26.361 --> 21:29.482
essentially to the industry these are the kinds of technologies we're

21:29.498 --> 21:33.521
interested in. However, if there happens to be some others that we may have previously

21:34.021 --> 21:37.798
eliminated because they were historically high water

21:38.298 --> 21:42.598
users, but now, for example, there's a closed-loop water system

21:42.825 --> 21:45.690
version of that now, we might consider that now at this point.

21:47.856 --> 21:51.640
And then an SMR facility is defined to not include any sort

21:52.140 --> 21:55.729
of long-term storage or waste or other hazardous materials

21:55.778 --> 21:58.495
as well, just to make that clear that there is no intent for those to

21:58.995 --> 22:01.088
be locations where long-term storage happens.

22:03.240 --> 22:06.749
With waivers, there's a couple of sections we just— Hey, Evan, just real quickly

22:07.249 --> 22:10.518
on that last point. Sure. Do we want to put some guardrails on

22:10.631 --> 22:15.309
what that timeframe looks like so that it's not an arbitrary what

22:15.809 --> 22:19.285
is long-term? I think maybe seeking out

22:19.510 --> 22:23.494
some help in determining what should be considered a short-term storage solution.

22:24.527 --> 22:28.801
We talking a few weeks, a month? I think he put a— didn't

22:29.301 --> 22:32.721
you put a deadline in? I thought he did. I'm trying to remember. I don't

22:33.221 --> 22:35.694
remember seeing a deadline. I don't think I did. I don't think I put a

22:36.194 --> 22:39.181
number on there. If it's there, when you get to it, I just— that was

22:39.229 --> 22:42.539
one of the questions that I had. Yeah, and I

22:43.039 --> 22:46.572
don't think I put a number on there only because it is a little bit

22:47.072 --> 22:51.048
variable depending on the technology that is used because the The

22:51.065 --> 22:54.153
fuels are different, and therefore the waste

22:54.653 --> 22:56.054
is also a little bit different in how it's handled.

22:57.796 --> 23:01.588
And so it's— that's something that certainly we can look

23:02.088 --> 23:06.479
at. Maybe it should be a waste removal plan. Yeah. Something that shows

23:06.496 --> 23:09.445
them that our intent is for it not to stay. Yep.

23:15.973 --> 23:17.020
I appreciate that.

23:19.363 --> 23:23.179
With the waivers, there was a piece of language on there that allowed for

23:23.404 --> 23:27.976
a potential waiver for water use assessments. And for clarification,

23:27.992 --> 23:31.217
it's not that we didn't care about water, it's that some technologies, for example like

23:31.717 --> 23:35.701
solar, may not necessarily— we expect there to be a huge water assessment

23:36.201 --> 23:38.670
on there. But we just removed that just to make sure that that always is

23:39.170 --> 23:42.460
going to happen so that we're not leaving that up to the industry

23:42.573 --> 23:46.509
to determine when a water assessment should be brought

23:47.009 --> 23:50.774
forward. And then there was other waivers throughout the language that

23:51.274 --> 23:53.737
was left up to the DRC, and that was switched over to City Council.

23:54.463 --> 23:58.076
Things like fencing and setbacks and whatnot. And that's now for City

23:58.576 --> 24:01.975
Council to review. This next one I want to spend

24:02.475 --> 24:07.618
a little bit more time with you on. As part of the motion

24:08.118 --> 24:11.887
when this was approved, there was a a request to have the AOZ

24:12.706 --> 24:16.333
application, the site plan application, the authority

24:16.833 --> 24:20.105
to approve that changed from the DRC to the City Council.

24:20.490 --> 24:24.085
So that effectively, um, creates that, um,

24:24.342 --> 24:27.648
the last major step, uh, where you're seeing all these different

24:28.148 --> 24:31.853
pieces come together. All the reviews have happened now at this point. You've now

24:31.966 --> 24:35.095
had, um, likely also federal and state reviews at

24:35.595 --> 24:39.608
this point. Now that's all that has happened, and now you have the City

24:40.108 --> 24:42.515
Council reviewing for that last final approval.

24:43.125 --> 24:46.193
And I'm happy to kind of get a little more background from you as far

24:46.693 --> 24:48.875
as why that's important to you. I think that's important for you to share.

24:50.208 --> 24:55.074
I wanted to just kind of bring up just my only concern with this is

24:55.250 --> 24:59.073
that what I understand from the industry is that when

24:59.121 --> 25:03.392
we get to this point, if we're trying to align The

25:03.892 --> 25:07.808
local, state, and federal at this point. So we're not getting ahead of federal licensing.

25:07.856 --> 25:11.501
We're not getting ahead of federal safety checks and things like that. We're trying

25:12.001 --> 25:15.146
to line this all up so everything happens at the same time. By the time

25:15.178 --> 25:18.229
you get to the AOZ site plan application to be approved,

25:18.855 --> 25:22.452
these companies have now spent millions, if not dozens of millions

25:22.484 --> 25:26.225
of dollars at this point, going through these applications and licensing processes.

25:26.739 --> 25:30.959
And so— I'm sorry, If there is a chance now that we're introducing

25:31.312 --> 25:35.291
that the City Council may ultimately deny the site plan application, that creates

25:35.323 --> 25:38.692
a lot of risk on them now where they may not want

25:39.192 --> 25:43.216
to entertain that risk with Eagle Mountain City and creates maybe

25:43.585 --> 25:47.644
a bit of a barrier that we don't want. I'm not saying that's necessarily

25:47.660 --> 25:50.388
how it's going to play out. This is up to you and as far as

25:50.888 --> 25:52.666
how you want to do that. But that was just the thing that came to

25:53.166 --> 25:57.317
my mind is what could happen if we go through with this one way

25:57.817 --> 26:00.328
or the other. So I just want to bring that to your attention and get

26:00.376 --> 26:04.004
your feedback on if you want to continue to proceed with this or if you

26:04.504 --> 26:06.366
want to think about this a little bit longer, how you want to handle this.

26:12.538 --> 26:17.668
I'll go. My thought is they're

26:17.684 --> 26:22.454
probably going to be in regular communication with the City Council individually.

26:22.711 --> 26:26.420
You know, it's not rare for a developer to reach out to me individually

26:26.982 --> 26:30.467
and have a conversation and reach out to council members. So I would imagine

26:30.499 --> 26:34.898
that they would have a pretty good pulse before they make that investment of

26:34.930 --> 26:38.864
time. But I feel like it's important for the city council to be

26:39.364 --> 26:42.910
that last stop so it's not moving through

26:42.943 --> 26:46.218
regulatory bodies and the people aren't being represented.

26:46.652 --> 26:49.832
from the council. So I support keeping that in,

26:50.250 --> 26:52.852
and that may have been my request to add that in there,

26:53.398 --> 26:57.173
that, you know, there's representation in the process, and I

26:57.673 --> 27:00.932
would think they would be pretty involved before they

27:01.432 --> 27:04.481
go to that great expense to make sure it's going to ultimately be approved.

27:04.947 --> 27:08.192
Absolutely. Yeah, for me, I would say that's the point.

27:08.722 --> 27:11.999
I mean, if I could put an exclamation point, that is

27:12.111 --> 27:15.886
the point. We want it to go through all of the rigorous processes So at

27:16.386 --> 27:19.661
the very end of that, that the residents understand that we haven't

27:20.161 --> 27:24.254
left anything uncovered and that it's going to come before us at the final aspect

27:24.754 --> 27:27.483
of it. And it's going to make them try to meet all of those requirements

27:27.983 --> 27:31.241
because it's a benefit to the users as well as the companies that are providing

27:31.741 --> 27:35.755
those. It's not something that is being done everywhere. And so I think that

27:36.044 --> 27:39.594
they understand that there is going to be a high level of scrutiny that's

27:40.094 --> 27:43.554
going to be happening to It really doesn't matter which state it is in,

27:43.842 --> 27:46.519
frankly, because this is a wide,

27:46.695 --> 27:50.637
countrywide issue that everybody's trying to address. I think the fact that we are

27:51.137 --> 27:55.717
willing to entertain it with some pretty significant guardrails should

27:55.765 --> 27:59.066
demonstrate to us that we've done our homework, or demonstrate to the citizens that we've

27:59.114 --> 28:02.415
done our homework, and that we have every intention to make sure

28:02.915 --> 28:06.437
that before we say yes to something, that we're not uncomfortable with it

28:06.485 --> 28:10.939
moving Absolutely.

28:10.988 --> 28:14.260
Yeah, I think transparency and education on this is absolutely key, and it doesn't even

28:14.760 --> 28:18.097
matter the energy type. One alternative

28:18.439 --> 28:22.014
recommendation I put on this slide I want to provide as well, just something to

28:22.514 --> 28:26.139
think about, is if by any means this

28:26.639 --> 28:29.823
gives you any pause in having this additional layer of approvals

28:29.871 --> 28:33.390
or whatnot, we could beef up the first 2 approvals

28:33.406 --> 28:36.773
that are happening. And so you have the rezone application,

28:37.206 --> 28:40.141
and then you also have the development agreement. And right now, in the language,

28:40.959 --> 28:44.423
uh, in the EAOZ right now, there's some pretty basic information that

28:44.923 --> 28:47.085
need— they need to be able to provide to kind of give you some context.

28:47.534 --> 28:51.254
But I think this is where a lot of places throughout Utah and other places

28:51.303 --> 28:55.135
across the country has really struggled with, is getting good information

28:55.248 --> 28:59.097
from the beginning. You see a lot of cities who get a lot

28:59.597 --> 29:02.961
of angry and upset residents because they're seeing applications go

29:03.461 --> 29:07.082
through, but a company is coming in with very little vague information

29:07.130 --> 29:09.471
as far as what's actually being prepared for, right?

29:10.305 --> 29:13.848
And so there's maybe a way to front-load a little

29:14.348 --> 29:17.279
bit more information to say, if you're going to come, don't come with a rezone

29:17.779 --> 29:21.833
application until you actually have something materially to show us as

29:21.865 --> 29:25.216
far as what's going to actually come down the line, so that when you get

29:25.716 --> 29:29.529
to the AEOZ site plan application, it really is more of a just a checking

29:29.642 --> 29:32.948
checkbox process because you've already seen so much

29:33.012 --> 29:36.671
and you've already reviewed so much through the first 2 steps already. So, I mean,

29:36.848 --> 29:40.523
let me ask this question, Evan. It sounds like you're suggesting that maybe there's

29:41.023 --> 29:44.728
some benefit in tying a site plan application to the rezone request

29:45.194 --> 29:48.387
and the development agreement, that those 3 have got to be merged on

29:48.420 --> 29:52.079
the front end for consideration. I think it's

29:52.175 --> 29:55.449
for you to be comfortable. If I were in your shoes,

29:55.706 --> 29:59.371
and even considering a rezone application to begin with,

30:00.126 --> 30:03.308
you'd want to know what's going here, right? And so I feel like you're going

30:03.808 --> 30:06.668
to want to know a lot of that information anyway at the beginning. And so

30:07.168 --> 30:10.574
hopefully by that point you get down to the site plan application,

30:10.622 --> 30:12.340
you should already know a whole lot about this.

30:14.430 --> 30:17.271
And so that's— I don't know that I would say they're,

30:17.613 --> 30:21.088
you know, 100% tied, but, you know, you would have a lot of the

30:21.120 --> 30:24.475
information much more up front. And so by the time you get to

30:24.975 --> 30:27.873
the site plan application approval,

30:28.643 --> 30:31.864
it's almost like a— it's a given that you're going

30:32.364 --> 30:34.958
to approve it. But it's kind of this chicken and egg thing, right? I mean,

30:34.990 --> 30:38.100
I'm listening to Councilmember Wood and he's saying, yeah, that's the point. We want to

30:38.116 --> 30:40.921
make sure that whatever it is that you're bringing, we've had a chance to look

30:41.049 --> 30:45.458
at it wholly. Yeah, not partially, not segmented,

30:46.195 --> 30:49.609
but wholly to say yes to it if we're going to say yes.

30:50.122 --> 30:52.750
And we don't want to find out after the fact that we said yes to

30:53.250 --> 30:57.156
this part, which compelled us to say yes to this part. We've dealt

30:57.656 --> 31:00.778
with that. We've all dealt with that. And so I think there's wisdom in what

31:01.278 --> 31:04.463
he's saying, but I also can see the risk for the development community who comes

31:04.963 --> 31:07.572
in and says, how in the world am I supposed to proceed if I don't

31:08.072 --> 31:09.687
have an assurance that I'm going to be able to do what I want to

31:09.751 --> 31:13.597
do? Yeah. That's the trick. I think that's the

31:14.097 --> 31:17.733
trick. And so as I'm listening, that's where my mind is going, is Maybe in

31:17.926 --> 31:21.570
this overlay zone specifically, we require

31:21.924 --> 31:25.809
a final site plan or a site plan that is considered final.

31:25.874 --> 31:28.266
It might have a few tweaks that they're going to have to come back and

31:28.766 --> 31:31.750
make, but maybe that's the way the council gets a chance to review that and

31:32.250 --> 31:35.764
see exactly what's happening on the front end. And the— and, and our residents get

31:36.264 --> 31:37.884
a chance to see that so there's no surprises.

31:38.703 --> 31:39.939
Yeah.

31:43.378 --> 31:49.019
So I understand what you're saying, but what assurances

31:49.519 --> 31:51.952
is that going to give to a developer if it can be all upended at

31:52.000 --> 31:52.580
the end anyway?

31:55.407 --> 31:58.603
What I'm saying is once that approval's been given for

31:58.684 --> 32:01.962
the site plan as part

32:02.462 --> 32:06.166
of a development agreement and a rezone application, all 3 of those have to

32:06.666 --> 32:10.147
go through simultaneously. Together jointly. They can't

32:10.647 --> 32:14.383
go through segmented. They have to go through as a package. No, I agree with

32:14.883 --> 32:17.238
that. I just didn't— maybe I didn't understand what you're saying with the final site

32:17.270 --> 32:20.687
plan approval. What do you mean by that then? Say it again,

32:21.040 --> 32:23.832
Councilor. I'm not sure I understand what you mean by the final site plan approval

32:23.928 --> 32:27.361
then. I understood that first part where it's all front-loaded, but what is

32:27.457 --> 32:30.522
the final site plan? What are you suggesting with that?

32:30.666 --> 32:34.099
Look, often once you have the preliminary site plan done,

32:34.532 --> 32:37.840
There are a few changes that have to happen to the final plat when they're

32:37.888 --> 32:41.678
working through it. They're usually pretty insignificant,

32:41.919 --> 32:45.499
and DRC handles those. Our staff handles those.

32:46.430 --> 32:49.449
So I just think that if we can get enough detail on

32:49.949 --> 32:52.452
the front end in a preliminary plat that says this is what we're going to

32:52.516 --> 32:56.578
build, and if there are any changes— I mean, if there are any changes

32:56.626 --> 32:59.709
that are considered substantive and we can define what that means,

33:00.689 --> 33:03.437
then they've got to come back through City Council approval. That's going to keep the

33:03.937 --> 33:07.293
development community on their toes so that they don't have to ask

33:07.793 --> 33:10.651
for those types of changes. They're going to make sure that they're working it out

33:11.151 --> 33:14.346
on the front end. And I don't know what's the best solution here. I'm just

33:14.846 --> 33:18.186
offering some perspective for consideration by my fellow council members.

33:19.198 --> 33:23.006
I'm thinking about, like, for me initially,

33:23.922 --> 33:27.455
there is no overlay without a development agreement. Like this particular

33:27.955 --> 33:31.291
one has to be tied to one. Um, I would say that for me,

33:31.307 --> 33:35.175
that's, that's got to be a must. But I think that also gives some

33:35.239 --> 33:38.818
flexibility. The moment that you move to a private contract

33:38.930 --> 33:42.397
with it, it gives some flexibility for the project. And I think

33:42.445 --> 33:45.767
that that should give the developer maybe a little

33:46.267 --> 33:50.983
bit of breathing room because they understand that we can talk about what are unique

33:51.483 --> 33:54.977
things to your project that we don't necessarily have the ability

33:55.477 --> 33:58.585
to bracket everything. That's why the development agreement's an

33:59.085 --> 34:01.199
important part of that, where we can kind of talk through some of those things.

34:01.953 --> 34:05.288
But to give them assurances up front— I don't think that they're

34:05.320 --> 34:09.618
naive in thinking that with a nuclear project that

34:09.794 --> 34:13.819
they aren't going to have to go through the paces. And I think they

34:14.319 --> 34:18.196
should understand that up front, that we are giving

34:18.453 --> 34:21.806
a path that has a green light. This path

34:21.838 --> 34:25.449
has some very specific brackets to it, some benchmarks to it.

34:25.835 --> 34:29.831
If you meet those things, you're likely to get an approval. But I think nobody

34:29.863 --> 34:33.426
else is really giving that. So I

34:33.458 --> 34:37.004
think we're going to have to go through this process at least once. And I

34:37.504 --> 34:39.460
may— I think maybe at the end of that, maybe we revisit and go,

34:39.476 --> 34:42.750
okay, we got it all wrong. We need to rethink this.

34:43.344 --> 34:45.639
But I think for the first one, it's better to be safe than sorry.

34:45.703 --> 34:48.815
Thank you. So Councilmember Wood, let me ask a question. I mean,

34:49.184 --> 34:52.472
great dialogue and, and, and I, and I share your passion with where you're

34:52.488 --> 34:55.792
at. I understand where you're coming from and I don't disagree with you, not,

34:55.856 --> 34:58.808
not in principle. What I'm, what I'm,

34:59.449 --> 35:02.224
I think what I'm trying to say and maybe what I'm envisioning is this.

35:02.352 --> 35:06.282
If we tie all 3 of those elements together, the preliminary plat, the development

35:06.782 --> 35:10.068
agreement, and the zoning approval, that they have to come together as a

35:10.132 --> 35:14.126
package. I think what that does is it requires The

35:14.142 --> 35:17.989
development community to bring forward a project that is being contemplated

35:18.489 --> 35:21.323
to be built. It's already been designed. They've already worked through that.

35:21.997 --> 35:25.203
And yeah, that's going to take a little more money on the front end,

35:25.283 --> 35:28.649
but that's where we're going to have the negotiation and try to figure it out

35:29.149 --> 35:32.192
and say, here's what we're going to be comfortable with. And if we give you

35:32.240 --> 35:36.183
an approval, then we're expecting you to build Development A because that's

35:36.683 --> 35:38.908
all we've looked at. We're not looking for Development B or C or for you

35:39.408 --> 35:42.519
to sell the property and change it. We're looking for you to build exactly what

35:43.019 --> 35:46.149
we've approved. Yeah, I would agree with that.

35:46.230 --> 35:49.571
I think that that gives— I mean, I think what we're looking for is the,

35:49.828 --> 35:53.361
the real picture of what they plan to do. And I think

35:53.861 --> 35:57.120
that our language in the development agreement, I mean, that's probably for Marcus.

35:57.297 --> 36:00.638
I think he hears us loud and clear on that. We don't want generalities,

36:00.670 --> 36:04.140
we don't want broad strokes, we want specificity with this

36:04.204 --> 36:08.055
particular thing. But you're right, if it's all up front,

36:08.376 --> 36:11.406
we see exactly what the project is, what equipment's going to be used,

36:11.887 --> 36:15.798
how they're going to deal with all the solutions that we've talked endlessly about— storage,

36:16.696 --> 36:20.495
hazardous material handling, you know, emergency procedures.

36:20.543 --> 36:23.509
I mean, all of those types of things, all of that's front-loaded. We have a

36:24.009 --> 36:27.084
chance to go through and say, okay, maybe now we want to have an expert

36:27.116 --> 36:30.947
come in that's a third party to all of this and help us to understand,

36:31.044 --> 36:34.815
is it enough? Right. Yeah, you don't even consider

36:35.315 --> 36:38.380
the rezone without knowing what it is that you're rezoning it for.

36:39.648 --> 36:43.711
And, and to Councilmember Hewish's point, ideally these approvals

36:43.823 --> 36:46.392
would not be the first time you're hearing about this. You know, there'd be lots

36:46.892 --> 36:50.615
of conversations and even community engagement that happens long beforehand,

36:51.193 --> 36:55.191
that the approvals are more after a long engagement with the public and

36:55.223 --> 36:58.843
with you, with experts and everything like that, so that you're you're fully aware of

36:58.859 --> 37:02.610
what you're actually looking at. And so you're not now trying

37:02.707 --> 37:05.814
to scramble on the— Well, and to Councilmember Hewish's points earlier,

37:06.087 --> 37:08.115
I mean, you say, look, they come in and they want to talk to me

37:08.615 --> 37:12.087
individually and they do that. All of that is going to have to transpire well

37:12.200 --> 37:16.156
in advance of anything ever landing here for us to consider

37:16.656 --> 37:19.279
an approval for. Okay.

37:22.275 --> 37:25.209
By the way, I thought you did a good job. your edits. I didn't get

37:25.709 --> 37:28.059
a chance to say that up front. I don't think so. But yeah, I went

37:28.559 --> 37:30.811
through, I went through this. I was happy with the things that you made the

37:31.311 --> 37:34.072
changes to. Um, I do think that, uh,

37:34.764 --> 37:38.944
maybe that added layer of scrutiny that Brett's talking about is— I agree

37:39.444 --> 37:40.118
with that. I think it's a good add.

37:43.954 --> 37:47.186
Okay, appreciate that. Um, the next thing

37:47.203 --> 37:50.269
is just on geothermal. Added some language to, uh,

37:50.286 --> 37:54.504
provide a report detailing efforts to protect the integrity of our aquifers in particular,

37:54.568 --> 37:56.883
including the levels, quality, and temperatures.

37:58.522 --> 38:02.465
I will say, just so you know, this is one area that recognized from Councilmember

38:02.965 --> 38:05.426
Wood last time that we're going to try and get some opportunities for all of

38:05.926 --> 38:09.348
us to learn more about geothermal. I will also say on that,

38:09.412 --> 38:13.341
I was actually just earlier today up at an energy conference,

38:14.101 --> 38:19.054
and geothermal is one of those things that's still evolving

38:19.648 --> 38:23.004
a little bit. And some of these things, USU and other

38:23.504 --> 38:26.954
universities here, University of Utah, they're very invested in trying

38:27.454 --> 38:30.903
to figure out geothermal in Utah, but there's still some unknowns. So you're right to

38:31.403 --> 38:34.676
be a little concerned about, you know, getting too ahead of ourselves right now.

38:34.740 --> 38:37.309
We don't want to necessarily just jump into it right now because there's still some

38:37.809 --> 38:41.644
unknowns. But it sounds like that geothermal

38:42.144 --> 38:44.422
will be a really good option for Utah. the long run.

38:45.482 --> 38:48.774
It's very clean. It is very safe. There's a lot of technology and a lot

38:49.274 --> 38:52.565
of stuff that goes into geothermal to make sure it

38:53.065 --> 38:56.259
is a great option for Utah. But there are still

38:56.759 --> 38:59.054
things to be fleshed out with that that we may need to tweak down the

38:59.554 --> 39:02.218
road. There are actually a couple proposals I have on that right now.

39:03.551 --> 39:06.795
I don't think we discussed— we had so much to look at

39:06.860 --> 39:10.138
when we first discussed this code, and I knew we'd be revisiting this,

39:10.154 --> 39:13.284
and this is one thing I, I think we need to talk about on geothermal.

39:13.332 --> 39:16.512
So when you look under generating unit, it defines

39:17.012 --> 39:19.217
some of the geothermal, you know,

39:21.310 --> 39:24.965
utilities that we'd be open to exploring, and it includes

39:25.465 --> 39:28.950
a couple that are higher water uses, and I think we should eliminate

39:29.450 --> 39:32.966
those. And, and if I understand the technology correctly, dry steam or flash

39:33.466 --> 39:37.797
steam, I think both of those are higher water water uses. I think

39:38.297 --> 39:42.787
for the time being it would be better to eliminate those and limit what

39:42.900 --> 39:48.282
we have in this overlay zone to binary

39:49.268 --> 39:53.003
or enhanced geothermal systems. From what

39:53.503 --> 39:54.417
I understand, both of those are closed loop.

39:56.795 --> 40:00.135
The flash and the dry steam would emit clouds into the air

40:00.587 --> 40:03.837
of steam. My preference would be to keep our

40:04.337 --> 40:07.542
skies free of steam clouds. Even though it is just steam, I think there's a

40:08.042 --> 40:11.471
stigma and sort of a branding that it comes with,

40:11.535 --> 40:15.288
and I'd prefer to not have that. So I think we should eliminate those and

40:15.304 --> 40:19.153
ensure whichever ones remain in the geothermal are ones that do not emit steam clouds.

40:19.618 --> 40:22.697
And according to my research, I think binary works, and then one called

40:23.197 --> 40:27.075
enhanced geothermal systems. Yep. I think that those would be acceptable.

40:28.937 --> 40:32.949
Can I ask a real quick question? I know you're on geothermal, but when

40:33.449 --> 40:37.491
it comes to low water— just to talk about steam, the low water use,

40:37.683 --> 40:40.941
high temperature— let's see, what was it called?

40:41.599 --> 40:44.745
Yeah, I had a question about that one. The low water use, high temperature

40:44.809 --> 40:48.115
gas-cooled reactors for SMRs, because I think

40:48.615 --> 40:51.887
those might emit steam. So I prefer— but I think there are some of

40:52.387 --> 40:55.819
those that would not. Right. And I would want to limit that as well to—

40:56.013 --> 40:59.844
if we are going with the high-temperature gas-cooled reactors, that they're not using

40:59.892 --> 41:02.268
a steam component in the emissions.

41:05.179 --> 41:08.810
Yeah, we'll have to specify in emissions because a

41:10.160 --> 41:12.650
lot of these will use a little bit of water even as part of a

41:13.150 --> 41:16.656
closed loop as part of the heat process internally, but not all of them will

41:17.110 --> 41:22.462
vent that to the atmosphere or anything like that. Councilmember Whiting,

41:22.510 --> 41:26.130
I just, I want to ask, is your— I appreciate,

41:26.178 --> 41:29.266
number one, that you did the research and that you're helping me understand a little

41:29.766 --> 41:32.370
more clearly. Let me start there. Okay, so thank you.

41:33.373 --> 41:36.947
And I'm wondering if your pushback is, or your concern is,

41:37.480 --> 41:40.858
just the visual impact of the steam, or is it the water consumption?

41:40.906 --> 41:43.766
I mean, is it both? It's both.

41:46.533 --> 41:49.568
So the visual, even if the water consumption is low,

41:49.873 --> 41:52.539
just the visual impact of that steam, you're like, I don't want to see that.

41:52.683 --> 41:56.521
That's my preference. But again, I guess we can look to see— look,

41:56.537 --> 41:59.828
I mean, there's the steam that the Linden plant puts off for natural gas,

41:59.844 --> 42:03.907
which is ginormous. And maybe there's a secondary turbine system

42:04.019 --> 42:08.370
that is designed to cool with water. They use about 3,000

42:08.402 --> 42:12.290
or about 1,000 acre-feet, I think they said. There will not be that

42:12.790 --> 42:15.600
type of water available in this valley to do something like that. Right, right.

42:15.728 --> 42:18.813
I guess I could be open to a steam cloud depending on what

42:19.313 --> 42:22.541
it looks like. Like, obviously if it's, if it's so minor that it's not really—

42:22.718 --> 42:25.836
That's the only reason I'm asking. Is it just a visual? You don't want to

42:25.852 --> 42:29.371
have to— you don't want people going, oh my gosh, they're emitting

42:29.804 --> 42:33.115
toxic air, right? Exactly. It becomes a stigma

42:33.615 --> 42:35.573
because you don't want to turn into Cedar Valley and be like, oh my gosh,

42:35.589 --> 42:38.817
it's an industrial That was the reason for my ask.

42:38.881 --> 42:42.655
I wanted to understand what your concerns are. So it's water use first and

42:43.155 --> 42:47.102
foremost. Correct. And I don't think that the council, at least a

42:47.231 --> 42:51.004
council today, tomorrow, or the next year, unless something changes

42:51.504 --> 42:54.665
dramatically in the water supply, is going to even have an option to do that.

42:54.713 --> 42:58.005
So let's start there. I just don't think there's enough water to

42:58.505 --> 43:02.629
support something like that. Set that

43:03.129 --> 43:06.757
aside, so even a small release of steam you

43:07.062 --> 43:09.728
would have some objections to just because of the way it looks? I'm trying to

43:10.228 --> 43:13.470
really pin you down. No, it is. I mean, I would prefer to keep the

43:13.970 --> 43:17.582
skies free of that, but there's— because there are technologies

43:18.082 --> 43:21.983
available that wouldn't allow— that could avoid that. But if it's

43:22.483 --> 43:25.452
a tiny amount of steam, it may be imperceptible. I mean, so that's something I

43:25.952 --> 43:27.877
guess, you know, again, there's going to be a grade on that.

43:29.680 --> 43:33.479
And because I don't know how to bracket this, I'm going to ask another question,

43:33.656 --> 43:37.051
okay? Okay. I sound like I'm advocating for this, and I don't know enough about

43:37.551 --> 43:38.894
it to advocate for it, so let me start there.

43:40.928 --> 43:43.482
And so my question is, as you look at that,

43:44.630 --> 43:49.464
can we reduce that by just simply stating something

43:49.561 --> 43:53.125
about the water use and keeping it to a bare minimum?

43:53.222 --> 43:54.729
I mean, I'm wondering if we can limit it to just that.

43:57.232 --> 43:59.691
I think that would be difficult to do. I don't know if it's feasible.

43:59.740 --> 44:03.460
And I guess it would be— because each of these categories,

44:03.525 --> 44:06.874
whether it's an SMR or some other of these, you know, geothermal,

44:07.308 --> 44:11.566
you know, how much— I don't know the right calculus for that. But that's

44:12.066 --> 44:15.451
why I think it's easier for me for the time being, given that there are

44:15.951 --> 44:19.090
numerous choices on the table for what kind of technologies, that we

44:19.155 --> 44:23.349
remove the technologies that emit steam. Unless— anyway,

44:23.365 --> 44:26.633
that's where I'd start. If developers come say, hey, here's a way for you guys

44:27.133 --> 44:30.079
to do it, and it's not going to create a visual impact issue, and it's

44:30.579 --> 44:33.380
the best technology because of ABCD, I guess we can have that discussion. Then you

44:33.880 --> 44:36.585
can address it in a development agreement. Yeah, in a development agreement. But I think

44:37.085 --> 44:40.559
just the openly, you know, sort of what we have in the code. I mean,

44:40.815 --> 44:44.420
I was looking at some of these geothermal and

44:44.500 --> 44:47.769
bringing up these reactors and pictures of them, and they're emitting a lot of steam.

44:48.624 --> 44:52.305
And I just, I think it becomes a visual aberration. And unfortunately,

44:52.321 --> 44:55.939
people will think that— they won't know it's steam, and then they'll feel this is

44:56.439 --> 44:59.756
an industrial complex as opposed to what we're trying

45:00.256 --> 45:03.618
to create. The answer is

45:03.715 --> 45:06.810
yes, you can do it. You can capture it

45:07.310 --> 45:11.049
so it's almost nothing that shows. And I would say, as far as the

45:11.549 --> 45:16.700
stigma goes, you're never going to overcome that no matter how great of a piece

45:17.200 --> 45:18.666
PR program you have, people are always going to make the assumption that we are

45:18.878 --> 45:22.557
polluting the air. They see any of that kind of thing going into the air,

45:22.637 --> 45:26.240
that's, that's going to be the stigma. I would ask the question,

45:26.578 --> 45:30.005
when you drive through North Salt Lake and you see coming off the gas power

45:30.119 --> 45:33.556
plants, is it clean? Is it not clean?

45:35.566 --> 45:38.873
Everybody's going to make the assumption it's not clean, whether it

45:39.373 --> 45:41.615
is or isn't. And I think for our city, I agree.

45:42.412 --> 45:46.394
How it looks, how it appears, is just as important as the

45:46.783 --> 45:50.279
bracketing that we're putting in place to make sure it doesn't actually get into the

45:50.779 --> 45:54.574
air. Because I don't particularly want our valley to be an industrial complex,

45:54.590 --> 45:57.704
nor to be viewed that way either. My personal opinion.

46:00.449 --> 46:04.570
Okay. With natural

46:05.070 --> 46:08.862
gas, we also added that Any natural gas operators

46:08.894 --> 46:12.488
would need to demonstrate air dispersion modeling to

46:12.988 --> 46:17.076
show there's no negative impacts. This should be very easy to follow

46:17.477 --> 46:20.991
just because they have to provide that anyway to get air quality permits. The same

46:21.491 --> 46:23.413
kind of language that we have in here is the same kind of things that

46:23.913 --> 46:27.184
they would need to deliver or produce for getting an air quality permit.

46:27.280 --> 46:30.761
So that would just be easy for us to be able to receive that as

46:30.858 --> 46:34.966
well rather than needing to wait until a public comment process

46:35.466 --> 46:38.739
or waiting until something we— where we grandma it from the state or whatever.

46:38.899 --> 46:41.774
We just want to see that at the same time. Is it okay if I

46:42.274 --> 46:45.756
ask a question, Mayor? So we've had different people reach out

46:45.884 --> 46:49.079
about an item that's later on the agenda, but it's related to natural gas.

46:49.802 --> 46:53.832
And their concern, or something that was stated, was please

46:54.332 --> 46:57.686
say no to this because we don't want to rely on whether or not the

46:58.186 --> 47:00.960
EPA has reduce their standards or not.

47:01.537 --> 47:05.003
And I feel like I remember when this came up before that

47:05.503 --> 47:09.206
Mayor Westmoreland said it wasn't that the standards were reduced, it's that the air quality

47:09.318 --> 47:13.169
in the valley improved enough that we weren't in the nonattainment

47:13.669 --> 47:17.548
anymore. Um, I'm gonna have a question later about that, but when

47:17.580 --> 47:21.302
it comes to this overlay zone, is there

47:21.431 --> 47:25.105
anything in the wording or how we can do that to

47:25.939 --> 47:29.375
address that concern that was raised by residents? I mean,

47:29.520 --> 47:32.876
a lot of that will get addressed at the state level before it ever comes

47:32.892 --> 47:36.938
to us. I mean, the state obviously has a strong desire,

47:37.484 --> 47:40.760
an incentive to ensure that we don't go

47:41.260 --> 47:45.031
into attainment, or I mean, even if we are in attainment, not make that worse

47:45.111 --> 47:47.616
in any way. Right. And we are,

47:48.804 --> 47:52.421
if we're not already crossing that line, Regardless of what Eagle Mountain has done

47:52.921 --> 47:57.493
or what have you, we're already right up against the line. Coming into attainment

47:57.993 --> 48:00.375
was, we just barely made it into attainment versus non-attainment.

48:02.320 --> 48:04.094
So it's not gonna take much for that.

48:06.398 --> 48:09.806
They're gonna have to be very thorough in providing all

48:10.306 --> 48:13.359
sorts of information as far as how many particulates and everything that

48:13.391 --> 48:16.305
they're providing to DEQ, DAQ,

48:16.727 --> 48:20.310
as far as what that air quality is. And so it's—

48:21.226 --> 48:24.070
there's, there's things that we can try and do on there, but I don't know

48:24.570 --> 48:27.734
that it would do anything more than just be redundant at that point. And for

48:28.234 --> 48:30.755
us, it's more of— I've tried to take the approach on this that it's more

48:30.819 --> 48:33.920
at least we should be properly informed much earlier

48:34.001 --> 48:37.037
on in the process. Right. Than anything.

48:37.295 --> 48:41.697
And I think that information that is provided

48:41.730 --> 48:45.146
to the state is really informative. It is really helpful to

48:45.162 --> 48:49.188
see in a scale, in a comparison sense,

48:49.493 --> 48:53.438
how this stacks up to other uses across the state and then where

48:53.938 --> 48:58.169
we actually expect to see any sort of air pollutants actually migrate throughout

48:58.669 --> 49:01.761
the valley, if that even happens at all. Got it. I think my key takeaway

49:02.261 --> 49:06.155
then is obviously not every city can have a mining

49:06.252 --> 49:09.764
expert and an air quality expert

49:10.264 --> 49:13.732
And that's where we have to rely on that state agency with the experts that

49:13.796 --> 49:17.473
actually work for it, that have that charge to help us guide

49:17.570 --> 49:20.861
our process. So if they're not receiving that approval

49:21.361 --> 49:24.811
from the state, it helps us understand that it won't work

49:25.311 --> 49:30.094
for Eagle Mountain either. Okay, thank you. Councilmember Clark, 1764090 under

49:30.594 --> 49:33.225
natural gas. This is a paragraph that he added, and I think this is really

49:33.725 --> 49:37.415
helpful. It says natural gas power plants may be approved only where the applicant demonstrates

49:37.915 --> 49:41.526
through site-specific air dispersion modeling and any health

49:41.574 --> 49:45.572
risk assessment required by the State Division of Air Quality or other governmental agency having

49:45.604 --> 49:49.217
jurisdiction that emissions from the proposed facility will comply with

49:49.717 --> 49:53.440
applicable state and federal ambient air quality standards. And then it goes

49:53.536 --> 49:57.294
on and lists some other criteria. So I think that that covers it pretty well.

49:57.904 --> 50:01.131
It's a concern. It should be, and I'm glad you raised it, but that's

50:01.163 --> 50:05.001
where I think he's addressed it. What point in the process

50:05.113 --> 50:08.405
is that, Brett? Is that in the very beginning of the DRC?

50:08.549 --> 50:11.520
Because that was actually one of the questions that I was going to ask.

50:12.114 --> 50:14.972
I think what we don't want to do is spend their money and get to

50:15.472 --> 50:18.633
the point where they get to a no because they don't have the proper

50:20.030 --> 50:23.659
credits or whatever they need to purchase. I don't know the answer to that.

50:23.723 --> 50:26.195
It's a good question. Councilmember Wood, because I mean,

50:27.254 --> 50:30.320
I think that's part of a due diligence on anybody that wants to consider

50:30.384 --> 50:33.289
building something like that. They have to know that they're going to be able to

50:33.789 --> 50:36.162
do it in this valley and have those air— I don't know what they call

50:36.662 --> 50:40.736
them, but the air quality attainment credits. Do they need that even if we're

50:40.816 --> 50:44.219
in an attainment area? None? And so they got to get them either way?

50:44.428 --> 50:48.183
Yes. Okay, so maybe that needs to be something

50:48.199 --> 50:51.233
that we prove in the very beginning Just like water shares.

50:51.892 --> 50:55.344
I, I feel like it does though, because anyone who's ever talked to us about

50:55.844 --> 50:59.278
any kind of natural gas and they've met and answered questions, I feel like they're

50:59.294 --> 51:02.826
always like— We absolutely said yes to somebody who said they were getting them

51:03.326 --> 51:07.322
and they didn't have them. Huh. Without going into who that was, that absolutely

51:07.402 --> 51:10.694
did happen. I do think too, on here, to answer your

51:11.194 --> 51:14.596
question, Councilmember Wood, it was in

51:16.137 --> 51:20.243
The applicant shall submit— it's in subsection C— the applicant

51:20.743 --> 51:24.815
shall submit the supporting analyses and agency determinations to

51:25.315 --> 51:28.746
the city and shall obtain and maintain all required air quality permits

51:29.246 --> 51:32.837
and approvals. And that's part of— may

51:33.337 --> 51:36.895
be approved only where the applicant demonstrates— so in C.

51:36.943 --> 51:42.029
So I think that it is part of that early determination.

51:42.550 --> 51:45.586
But maybe there's something that we can do to tighten that up a little bit.

51:47.042 --> 51:50.637
Evan can be a wordsmith for that. But I

51:50.686 --> 51:54.329
agree that it's important to have the credits secured

51:54.829 --> 51:58.120
before anything else. Yeah, I mean— I

51:58.169 --> 52:00.940
would say just before final approval. Yeah.

52:01.884 --> 52:05.466
So. Yeah, I know the industry norm is that

52:05.966 --> 52:09.847
they're usually getting the credits beforehand. But to make sure we're

52:10.347 --> 52:15.061
catching everything, we can ask to see evidence of that.

52:15.686 --> 52:19.504
I was just gonna say that the data center playbook is nat gas,

52:19.777 --> 52:24.173
single cycle, closed loop. Like, Meta's paving

52:24.189 --> 52:27.397
the way. They have figured out the model. So I think we'd be naive

52:27.897 --> 52:33.060
if we're sitting here thinking that these projects are going to lead with

52:33.109 --> 52:36.398
nuclear or solar, or they're gonna

52:36.898 --> 52:40.736
come in with nat gas and try to get the valley to capacity to

52:41.236 --> 52:44.030
where the state says, okay, you're full. And I think we just need to be

52:44.530 --> 52:47.838
careful of that mindset and make sure

52:47.902 --> 52:50.971
that we're communicating, you know, that that's not our preference

52:52.208 --> 52:55.859
in every case. I would remind too, just in case it's been forgotten too,

52:55.875 --> 53:00.028
we did put into this AEOZ a cap

53:00.528 --> 53:03.150
on the total amount of natural gas that can be put into this overlay zone.

53:03.214 --> 53:06.565
So there's 2 gigawatts. You can reduce that or increase that however you

53:06.581 --> 53:10.878
want, but right now it's 2 gigawatts worth. So just

53:11.378 --> 53:14.707
keep in mind too. And with Williams, I think that's

53:15.207 --> 53:18.890
289 megawatts. So we're 1/8 of

53:19.390 --> 53:22.541
the way there. Is that right? And do we know with the 2 gigawatts,

53:24.300 --> 53:27.794
I know in Delta, That power plant is at 2 gigawatts.

53:27.843 --> 53:31.889
That's a pretty big facility. Do we know how

53:31.905 --> 53:35.636
that would fit within the state code? Would we be well below the state

53:36.136 --> 53:39.477
requirements? I don't know that. We'd just be speculating. Maybe we don't

53:39.509 --> 53:41.387
know. Yeah, I don't know that. Okay.

53:43.701 --> 53:47.438
Should that number be in direct correlation with the air credit requirement?

53:48.553 --> 53:52.169
We could try to see if there's a way to do that. We can talk

53:52.669 --> 53:55.813
with the state. And maybe go 50% less than that.

53:56.920 --> 54:00.066
Well, and just for context, I mean, when we look at

54:00.130 --> 54:03.966
something later on the agenda, the ENYO, when you look at theirs, they're at full

54:04.047 --> 54:07.738
build-out, they're looking at a 3-gigawatt power generation

54:08.238 --> 54:11.799
facility. And I think 600 megawatts

54:11.864 --> 54:15.347
is what they're intending to do with natural gas. And then

54:16.406 --> 54:19.102
the other's broken down, 1,200 and 1,200, and I can't remember.

54:19.440 --> 54:22.579
Solar and I think BESS, I think battery energy storage systems.

54:22.627 --> 54:26.008
So it's just important to keep that in, in context

54:26.169 --> 54:29.919
as far as what's happening further south of where we're

54:30.419 --> 54:34.209
at. You make a good point because that's outside

54:34.709 --> 54:38.307
of our municipal boundaries, and maybe that's one of those things we probably need to

54:38.807 --> 54:42.411
have a conversation with the adjoining neighbors

54:43.537 --> 54:47.153
so that there is a standard for the valley. that we're falling under so

54:47.217 --> 54:50.716
that we're not hurting each other. Well, I mean, and recognize

54:50.764 --> 54:54.199
that it's outside of our municipal boundaries today, but it may

54:54.699 --> 54:57.168
not be forever that way, right? I mean, we've, we've,

54:58.131 --> 55:02.063
we've, we've adjusted our annexation plan to

55:02.563 --> 55:05.659
include some of those areas where this would, would be located. So I just,

55:05.675 --> 55:09.414
it's just context I think that I want to make sure is in every council

55:09.430 --> 55:15.099
member's mind as we move forward and talk Okay.

55:15.503 --> 55:18.919
Besides that, uh, we had, uh, decommissioning bond requirements was added

55:19.419 --> 55:22.944
to natural gas. And then on coming back

55:23.444 --> 55:26.840
to nuclear again, just real quick, um, we did require

55:26.952 --> 55:30.317
that any description of any hazardous waste chemicals and materials

55:30.349 --> 55:33.014
that are meant to be kept on site for more than 24 hours need to

55:33.514 --> 55:36.374
be, um, something that we're notified of,

55:36.471 --> 55:40.279
that we're kept in the loop as far as what's on that site. Part of

55:40.779 --> 55:42.997
that for safety response and other things.

55:45.276 --> 55:48.689
And then I already kind of mentioned this earlier, but we're opening

55:49.189 --> 55:53.067
the door to older technologies only that have been modernized in some way, so that

55:53.100 --> 55:56.656
there's a closed-loop system or there's some sort of modernization effort that has happened.

55:57.047 --> 56:00.509
Would that be the appropriate place to have them submit a removal plan?

56:02.053 --> 56:05.225
Sorry, say it again. Would that be the appropriate place to have them submit a

56:05.725 --> 56:07.984
removal plan? So it does state that if it's going to be there for over

56:08.484 --> 56:11.966
24 hours, they need to notify us. But perhaps that's the place to say,

56:12.158 --> 56:17.056
and a removal plan within— and

56:17.556 --> 56:20.315
I think that's probably where experts need to come in and help us with that,

56:20.428 --> 56:23.735
what that timeframe ought to be. But it ought to be short.

56:25.180 --> 56:28.504
Yeah, I think it'll depend on what it is. And I don't

56:29.004 --> 56:31.843
know how we delineate that right now. But, you know, say for example, nuclear fuel

56:32.343 --> 56:35.787
itself. They're going to store some fuel on site before it actually goes into the

56:36.235 --> 56:39.394
reactor, and then when it comes off or out of the reactor,

56:39.602 --> 56:43.722
it is going to stay on site temporarily, ideally for

56:44.222 --> 56:47.297
not very long, right? And then it does need to go. I don't think it's

56:47.797 --> 56:50.920
realistic that it's going to stay there for only, you know, a week. It might

56:51.420 --> 56:54.447
take some time to arrange transportation. No, I think it'll be probably in months,

56:54.591 --> 56:58.216
right? But not years. But there might be There might be some other types

56:58.716 --> 57:01.396
of hazardous waste or chemicals. I don't know. It depends on what the technology is.

57:01.974 --> 57:05.843
That might be things that we're just not considering. I would say this is hazardous

57:05.908 --> 57:09.697
waste that we're talking about, not hazardous materials, because hazardous

57:10.197 --> 57:13.775
materials are what they're going to use to generate. The waste is their spent.

57:14.353 --> 57:16.682
We don't want them just sitting there deteriorating. Yeah.

57:17.789 --> 57:21.161
Yeah. And we can certainly ask for that. That is going to be part of

57:21.980 --> 57:25.468
both the processes of the Nuclear Regulatory Commission and the Department of

