02:04:17.167 --> 02:04:21.036
footprint. And so I think that that's probably played into some

02:04:21.536 --> 02:04:24.971
of this as well. And so it's a determination.

02:04:25.036 --> 02:04:28.907
Are we going to make them go back to their lot line? Are we going

02:04:29.407 --> 02:04:32.632
to— are we willing and open to sell it. And do we need these long

02:04:32.728 --> 02:04:36.759
corridors? I don't think we need all of it long-term. But I do agree

02:04:37.259 --> 02:04:40.741
that we need to— if we are going to hang

02:04:40.757 --> 02:04:43.791
on to this property, we need to come up with a mechanism and a plan

02:04:43.888 --> 02:04:47.372
on how we're going to develop it. I do agree with that.

02:04:48.400 --> 02:04:51.820
Okay. And we're probably not going to fill it with grass and sprinklers,

02:04:52.093 --> 02:04:57.590
is my guess. And so we're left with just a dirt Yeah,

02:04:58.090 --> 02:05:01.150
I think that's the tough point, right? It's, uh,

02:05:01.650 --> 02:05:03.390
we know, yeah, we're not going to be able to fill it with turf.

02:05:03.570 --> 02:05:06.010
We know we're not going to be able to irrigate it. We know we're not

02:05:06.510 --> 02:05:08.530
going to be able to keep up with those maintenance costs. So what does it

02:05:09.030 --> 02:05:12.730
turn into? Is it, is it a corridor with amenities in it? Is it,

02:05:13.230 --> 02:05:15.350
is there a bunch of xeriscape in it? Is it just a trail and we

02:05:15.850 --> 02:05:19.230
need to slim them down? But yeah, I do agree that we need to look

02:05:19.730 --> 02:05:22.822
at a bigger master plan. Thank Mr. you. Plummer. And I think there's an opportunity

02:05:22.855 --> 02:05:25.942
here to where we can achieve— I think for the most part

02:05:25.990 --> 02:05:29.978
this council is in favor of big lots within our city,

02:05:31.746 --> 02:05:34.585
is in favor of big lots within our city, so it achieves that, but it

02:05:34.650 --> 02:05:37.877
also narrows that space down to where

02:05:37.893 --> 02:05:41.330
it's not so big and unmanageable. I just don't know what you do with

02:05:41.830 --> 02:05:45.038
that space. It's not big enough for a frisbee golf course,

02:05:45.673 --> 02:05:48.672
but you want to keep it wide enough that you could come in and have

02:05:49.172 --> 02:05:52.899
an appetite to xeriscape it and make it nice. Today it feels pretty wide.

02:05:53.012 --> 02:05:56.598
Like, that's a lot of dirt space

02:05:56.809 --> 02:05:59.965
that we just haven't done anything, probably because it's not a project

02:06:00.465 --> 02:06:03.867
that we've been willing to tackle. Mayor,

02:06:03.883 --> 02:06:07.260
is it okay if I say something? I agree

02:06:07.760 --> 02:06:10.605
with the stuff that's been said. I think we do need to take a long,

02:06:10.702 --> 02:06:13.977
hard look at all of the trails and all of the open space in

02:06:14.477 --> 02:06:18.186
our city. And it's been a continuous process. I like the idea of having

02:06:18.219 --> 02:06:22.207
the Community Services Board take a look at it. They have already been

02:06:22.320 --> 02:06:25.112
kind of discussing some of these things when we were talking about trails.

02:06:26.759 --> 02:06:30.568
But I also think that we should consider selling this piece of land

02:06:30.958 --> 02:06:33.120
because it's a pretty expansive piece,

02:06:34.111 --> 02:06:36.777
as Council Member Hewish said.

02:06:37.626 --> 02:06:41.666
And the fact is, is if we're going to have finished

02:06:41.714 --> 02:06:44.391
or improved trails, we have to pay for them somehow.

02:06:45.161 --> 02:06:48.864
And we want to be fiscally conservative. We don't want to,

02:06:49.345 --> 02:06:53.000
you know, go crazy with our spending. So one of the ways

02:06:53.032 --> 02:06:56.575
that we can do this, because it's not impact fee eligible,

02:06:57.345 --> 02:07:01.272
is for us to sell some of that land and put

02:07:01.772 --> 02:07:05.040
it into Fixing up the areas around it.

02:07:05.136 --> 02:07:08.743
We can still have epic trails throughout our entire city,

02:07:09.544 --> 02:07:13.119
but what they're asking to take on here isn't

02:07:13.167 --> 02:07:16.598
a ton, and it still preserves a really good chunk

02:07:17.098 --> 02:07:19.885
of land to be an improved trail space.

02:07:21.311 --> 02:07:24.886
Question, the, the proceeds from these sales, can they be restricted to

02:07:24.999 --> 02:07:28.285
improve the area around that path, or does it just

02:07:28.785 --> 02:07:32.237
drop into the general fund. I think the previous ones

02:07:32.253 --> 02:07:35.901
we said specifically went to parks in some cases, but it's

02:07:36.401 --> 02:07:38.726
a— that's council. I mean, it's a budget issue, so if you wanted to restrict

02:07:39.226 --> 02:07:41.391
them, you could. Okay.

02:07:44.043 --> 02:07:47.600
I think everybody's mostly spoke, and I don't

02:07:47.616 --> 02:07:49.799
get a vote on this. I was trying to keep my mouth shut, but I

02:07:50.299 --> 02:07:53.118
want to weigh in anyway just for some context because of some of this.

02:07:54.392 --> 02:07:57.004
And first of all, I love teasing you, and it's mostly because we get along.

02:07:57.420 --> 02:08:00.673
You literally opened the meeting with your whole goal is to preserve open space,

02:08:01.827 --> 02:08:05.592
and now you're open, willing to— at least you sound like

02:08:05.608 --> 02:08:10.031
maybe willing to sell this. So— Well, yes, we— there

02:08:10.175 --> 02:08:13.780
is plenty of open space in this corridor. I mean, we're looking at a very

02:08:14.280 --> 02:08:17.770
specific area, and it's been here how long? When was your

02:08:18.270 --> 02:08:22.113
home built? In '99. In 1999. Have you seen any No,

02:08:22.177 --> 02:08:25.644
and we moved in in 2012. And when it gets

02:08:26.144 --> 02:08:29.640
windy, you get a lot of dirt. Yeah. And dust. Exactly. And you're asking yourself,

02:08:29.656 --> 02:08:32.625
when are they going to improve this freaking area? I'll bet.

02:08:33.733 --> 02:08:36.782
There's— these exist all throughout the city. So I'm trying to find it, strike a

02:08:37.282 --> 02:08:40.907
balance between having a nice xeriscape open

02:08:41.407 --> 02:08:45.721
space and improve large lots. That's my thought.

02:08:45.850 --> 02:08:48.658
How do we find balance in this situation? And also give you space,

02:08:48.770 --> 02:08:52.316
there's about 40 feet that

02:08:52.588 --> 02:08:56.839
would be left on each side of that path. So 40

02:08:57.339 --> 02:09:00.433
feet of left, of open space. Now I wanna bring this in. On this side,

02:09:00.770 --> 02:09:04.107
80 feet. Yeah, 80 total, which is why I

02:09:04.219 --> 02:09:07.235
wanted to weigh in because there's only been one other time that we've approved a

02:09:07.283 --> 02:09:11.775
sale and it was for somewhere between 7

02:09:12.275 --> 02:09:15.588
and 10 feet to the largest one being 20 feet. Only because it was

02:09:15.653 --> 02:09:18.709
a straight line and only because of encroachments. This one

02:09:19.209 --> 02:09:22.670
right here, the distance is twice or 3

02:09:23.170 --> 02:09:26.047
times bigger. If we measured this the other day in a meeting,

02:09:27.193 --> 02:09:30.454
is it 40 feet? Is that what you're asking?

02:09:30.502 --> 02:09:33.556
Yeah, it's like 40 feet from the path to that orange line.

02:09:34.482 --> 02:09:38.625
I'm talking about from your property line to the— how much are you asking for?

02:09:39.031 --> 02:09:42.182
It's 40 feet. About the same. And the other one we did 10.

02:09:42.729 --> 02:09:46.312
So that's a way bigger ask

02:09:46.812 --> 02:09:49.847
than what we've done on the other trails, and they wanted more and we didn't

02:09:49.863 --> 02:09:54.892
allow it. So really what my comment is, is I'm not 100—

02:09:55.262 --> 02:09:58.090
I have taken a stand that I'm against selling our open space, and I'm going

02:09:58.590 --> 02:10:01.352
to stay consistent with that. I'm not completely against this.

02:10:01.994 --> 02:10:05.304
My issue is until we decide what the rest

02:10:05.804 --> 02:10:09.126
of it's going to be How do we know what we need? So my preference

02:10:09.626 --> 02:10:12.370
would be, tell me what we're going to do with all these open spaces,

02:10:12.434 --> 02:10:15.629
because we haven't been able to say that. Then let's sell what's left.

02:10:15.725 --> 02:10:19.386
If we sell this first, then we're then we're boxing ourselves

02:10:19.402 --> 02:10:23.063
in. And I don't know why it's it's okay to give forty feet on

02:10:23.175 --> 02:10:26.081
this side and nothing to the other side. If we're going to do that,

02:10:26.097 --> 02:10:28.811
I'd say twenty and twenty. I mean, so to me,

02:10:29.373 --> 02:10:32.266
it's a little bit unfair, especially at forty. feet. If,

02:10:32.816 --> 02:10:36.115
if it's to true up a, uh, an encroachment,

02:10:36.778 --> 02:10:39.139
then do 5 feet all the way so that we can just true up some

02:10:39.639 --> 02:10:42.795
encroachment. That's really what Todd years ago was trying to,

02:10:43.345 --> 02:10:46.726
to, uh, clean up. So that, that to

02:10:47.226 --> 02:10:50.935
me is— this seems like it's just a lot to—

02:10:51.519 --> 02:10:54.277
until we know what we're doing. I'm just going to say that that's an arbitrary

02:10:54.777 --> 02:10:58.641
argument. There's, there's no, there's no data to even To back

02:10:59.141 --> 02:11:02.627
up what you just said. It's an— it's a personal opinion of you can't

02:11:03.127 --> 02:11:06.067
have this much. It might— it had nothing to do with how much. I said,

02:11:06.099 --> 02:11:08.899
what are we going to do with it? No, you said you get 40 and

02:11:09.399 --> 02:11:12.586
the other got 7 or 10. That's your words. What does

02:11:12.602 --> 02:11:14.759
that have to do with that? This is a separate application.

02:11:17.149 --> 02:11:20.832
They're not connected. Okay, finish your statement, because what I said— or

02:11:20.880 --> 02:11:23.835
let me clarify what I said then so that you can hear it. What I

02:11:24.335 --> 02:11:27.838
said is until we know what we're doing with that, I don't know how much

02:11:27.935 --> 02:11:31.501
we can sell. The rest of my comment is this: how many times are we

02:11:32.001 --> 02:11:33.784
going to have this discussion and do nothing?

02:11:35.692 --> 02:11:39.000
I've been here for 2 and a half years. We've had this discussion 4

02:11:39.500 --> 02:11:43.567
or 5 times. So who is it that's going to magically decide what

02:11:43.664 --> 02:11:46.971
we're going to do with these open spaces between houses while the

02:11:47.471 --> 02:11:50.750
residents continue to have to deal with the fire hazards behind them? They have to

02:11:51.250 --> 02:11:54.658
deal with the thistles behind them, and it's their path that they're walking through,

02:11:54.723 --> 02:11:58.341
that their kids are walking to their houses. Where's the compassion

02:11:58.760 --> 02:12:02.093
there? And they're offering money to

02:12:02.593 --> 02:12:06.263
solve part of that problem, not come take the whole thing. 40 feet on either

02:12:06.763 --> 02:12:08.694
side is still 80 feet we can't take care of.

02:12:10.546 --> 02:12:14.555
So what's the consternation about this? They want to improve their lots

02:12:15.055 --> 02:12:18.325
by adding an additional 40 feet to the back side of it. They're going to

02:12:18.825 --> 02:12:22.180
pay us for it, and that's land we

02:12:22.680 --> 02:12:24.750
no longer have to take care of that we don't have the money to take

02:12:25.250 --> 02:12:28.380
care of. So I want to ask one question. Are you willing to give 40

02:12:28.880 --> 02:12:32.299
feet to the other side as well? They're not asking for it. I think

02:12:32.799 --> 02:12:34.387
they would if they had the opportunity. They're not asking for it, so we don't

02:12:34.452 --> 02:12:38.339
make up scenarios when there isn't another scenario to, to look at.

02:12:38.403 --> 02:12:41.134
But it opens up a discussion though. So let them come ask.

02:12:41.616 --> 02:12:45.110
Well, and that's, and that's where I think At least my opinion is that's why

02:12:45.610 --> 02:12:48.370
I'd like to see the Community Services Board go in there and say we're going

02:12:48.870 --> 02:12:50.430
to use this or we're not going to use this, and if we're not going

02:12:50.930 --> 02:12:52.690
to use it, how much are we willing to give up? And do a nice

02:12:53.190 --> 02:12:56.650
little plan that incorporates like looking at that whole thing and come back with a

02:12:57.150 --> 02:13:00.590
recommendation, which may be to sell. So I'm not saying I'm not against selling it;

02:13:01.090 --> 02:13:03.270
I'm just saying I want them to create come create a plan, and then we

02:13:03.770 --> 02:13:06.460
can we can say okay this this is the land we're willing to give up,

02:13:06.690 --> 02:13:09.925
and then and then if the neighbors want to come. forward and say, yeah,

02:13:10.149 --> 02:13:13.600
yep, we're good, we'll come together and we'll buy this. And because otherwise— so

02:13:14.100 --> 02:13:16.713
for instance, if we go forward with this one, if you— as you proceed north,

02:13:16.905 --> 02:13:19.361
now it's misaligned with the corridor to the north.

02:13:20.179 --> 02:13:23.886
Um, and, and so it, it's not the same width. So the north,

02:13:23.950 --> 02:13:27.256
you have— we have one home, and then there's Harrier Street that goes up straight

02:13:27.756 --> 02:13:30.899
through into the new neighborhood. So it's kind of boxed in

02:13:31.284 --> 02:13:35.023
somewhat. But then on the north side of Harrier is again at

02:13:35.523 --> 02:13:38.776
the same corridor that opens up to the field between the high school

02:13:39.276 --> 02:13:42.432
and our neighborhood. Yeah. No, so anyway,

02:13:42.674 --> 02:13:45.702
to everyone's points here, like, the city's done nothing about this for decades.

02:13:46.378 --> 02:13:49.903
And I think, at least my opinion, and I could get overruled, you know,

02:13:50.064 --> 02:13:53.480
by— overvoted, I think the Community Services Board should take a

02:13:53.980 --> 02:13:57.357
handful of weeks, study it, come up with a plan, and their

02:13:57.389 --> 02:14:00.816
plan may be sell it. Or their

02:14:01.316 --> 02:14:03.240
plan may be— Let's say they come up with a plan. How are you going

02:14:03.740 --> 02:14:05.840
to pay for that, though? I mean, the reality of it is, is we're not

02:14:06.340 --> 02:14:09.820
talking about 16 feet. It depends on what their plan

02:14:10.320 --> 02:14:14.320
is. And their plan may— it just depends

02:14:14.820 --> 02:14:16.340
on what they come back with. I'm sure they're not going to put a theme

02:14:16.840 --> 02:14:20.800
park in there. You know, it's going to be something simpler. But what they

02:14:21.300 --> 02:14:25.080
recommend is probably going to be less than what we've got.

02:14:25.580 --> 02:14:29.525
I can't imagine they're going to use every foot of that. I do foresee this

02:14:29.990 --> 02:14:32.478
being able to sell. I just want to say that— I just want to see

02:14:33.024 --> 02:14:36.877
a plan. Let's say that they decide they're

02:14:37.377 --> 02:14:41.468
going to do the 40 feet on the other side. What's lost? 40 feet

02:14:41.532 --> 02:14:44.694
on the side that they're proposing, 40 feet on the other side. You still have

02:14:45.194 --> 02:14:49.061
40 feet to take care of that we can't take care of.

02:14:50.200 --> 02:14:53.892
And if you want to, you know, take out your shovel and make a new

02:14:54.392 --> 02:14:57.181
line with the path up there. Now you put 20 feet on either side of

02:14:57.681 --> 02:15:01.030
it. We'll use an 8-foot path just for kicks and giggles.

02:15:01.615 --> 02:15:05.178
You still have 14 feet that we can't

02:15:05.678 --> 02:15:09.058
take care of. And we— people come and say to us, what's your solution to

02:15:09.558 --> 02:15:12.000
this? How are you going to solve this problem? Oh, well, we're going to pass

02:15:12.500 --> 02:15:14.335
it off to somebody that's not going to do anything with it. We've done that

02:15:14.835 --> 02:15:17.421
for 2 and a half years. The same thing happened when I was on the

02:15:17.921 --> 02:15:21.012
Planning Commission. I'm asking the same question. It's very easy to Oh,

02:15:21.093 --> 02:15:23.245
well, we're going to come up with a plan. We're going to, you know,

02:15:23.807 --> 02:15:26.522
we don't like your plan. We don't have a plan, but we don't like yours.

02:15:27.196 --> 02:15:30.569
And I just feel like— Sorry. At some point,

02:15:31.372 --> 02:15:35.308
we have to say we have to sell some of this land

02:15:35.808 --> 02:15:39.211
because we don't have the capability of taking care of it behind houses. Now,

02:15:39.227 --> 02:15:42.600
I'm not saying that we sell all of our open space. It doesn't

02:15:43.100 --> 02:15:45.989
get rid of the path. We're getting rid of a huge expanse of land we

02:15:46.489 --> 02:15:49.616
can't take care of. I— so

02:15:50.162 --> 02:15:54.014
I hear the request for a plan, but the reality

02:15:54.514 --> 02:15:58.251
of it is, is even if the Community Services Board spent 6

02:15:58.751 --> 02:16:02.440
months coming up with a plan, we're decades away from being able to pay

02:16:02.745 --> 02:16:06.259
to do what the plan would be. There's probably 200

02:16:06.356 --> 02:16:10.143
of these strips in City Center that the

02:16:10.643 --> 02:16:13.113
same problem is, and if it costs us what,

02:16:13.675 --> 02:16:16.857
$50,000? Like, for every single one?

02:16:16.969 --> 02:16:20.713
That's an exorbitant amount of money that, that we just

02:16:20.810 --> 02:16:24.682
aren't prepared to do. And I don't see a city council anytime

02:16:24.698 --> 02:16:29.165
soon saying we're going to fix up all these paths at

02:16:29.665 --> 02:16:32.958
the peril of something else in the city budget. So I'm at the

02:16:33.458 --> 02:16:37.184
point where I'm in agreeance with Council Member Wood that I

02:16:37.296 --> 02:16:41.034
think we should initiate this process. I think we should come

02:16:41.066 --> 02:16:44.482
up with the options of a standardized option, so when

02:16:44.579 --> 02:16:47.866
people come, here are your options for paying for it

02:16:48.366 --> 02:16:51.827
and dealing with it. And I think that it should be very specific.

02:16:52.453 --> 02:16:56.190
Look at what's there. If there's enough remaining land,

02:16:56.607 --> 02:17:00.039
then I think we should move forward with the sale, because we can

02:17:00.071 --> 02:17:04.208
use that money to help pay for improvements and

02:17:04.418 --> 02:17:08.063
No one else has said this, but as each of those lots increase in size,

02:17:08.496 --> 02:17:11.756
they also increase in the amount of property tax that comes into our city.

02:17:12.189 --> 02:17:15.208
And not that I expect each little homeowner to be able to do that,

02:17:15.625 --> 02:17:19.045
but it's better to have bigger lots

02:17:19.318 --> 02:17:22.834
and additional size and money coming into the coffers so that

02:17:22.866 --> 02:17:26.640
we can pay for those services that we've been struggling with all year to

02:17:27.140 --> 02:17:29.899
pay for. So like, I hear everything, but again,

02:17:30.171 --> 02:17:34.034
This agenda item is just initiating the process of

02:17:34.098 --> 02:17:37.801
whether or not we'll make the decision. So I'd like to hear an explanation.

02:17:37.849 --> 02:17:40.702
How do you feel it's fair to sell 40 feet here?

02:17:41.376 --> 02:17:45.303
Because the mayor brought up this question. On the last land disposal that was

02:17:45.803 --> 02:17:48.862
along this, you know, in this similar area, there was much less.

02:17:49.342 --> 02:17:52.532
So why was that— why were those citizens not afforded

02:17:53.032 --> 02:17:56.700
the opportunity to have more? Why was the city council so tight on how

02:17:57.200 --> 02:18:00.603
much was I, I think it's less about what the city council did

02:18:00.956 --> 02:18:04.103
and what was requested, and it doesn't mean that they can't come in

02:18:04.603 --> 02:18:07.346
and get something else. The other piece is, in my neighborhood,

02:18:07.683 --> 02:18:11.200
there's no paths down the back of houses. Our yards just

02:18:11.216 --> 02:18:14.491
butt up against each other. Should I be mad that the city council in

02:18:14.989 --> 02:18:18.859
2001 didn't plan ahead and require

02:18:19.359 --> 02:18:21.797
a path behind my house? Can I answer that? Because you got a park,

02:18:21.813 --> 02:18:24.559
and this does not have a path. developer didn't finish their park. That's what that

02:18:25.059 --> 02:18:28.702
is. That is a poor excuse for a park, and we're an embarrassment as

02:18:28.718 --> 02:18:32.684
a city that we're considering that open space and doing absolutely

02:18:33.184 --> 02:18:36.746
nothing with it. And you know what's crazy is I'm turning into Rich Wood

02:18:37.246 --> 02:18:42.542
right now. I'm just— so

02:18:42.847 --> 02:18:46.316
I just think that, please, I'm asking my fellow council

02:18:46.816 --> 02:18:50.136
members to Say yes, let's initiate this process.

02:18:50.425 --> 02:18:54.520
Simultaneous to that, let's request that the Community Services Board

02:18:55.020 --> 02:18:57.683
take a look at it and come back and give us some suggestions.

02:18:58.116 --> 02:19:01.713
And let's start moving forward with this and bring in the money we need

02:19:02.213 --> 02:19:05.663
to fix things up. Yeah, I think it, in a

02:19:05.727 --> 02:19:09.565
budget-neutral way, if we could take the sales proceeds, dedicate them

02:19:09.757 --> 02:19:14.655
to that, the rest of that space, whether it's Xeriscape

02:19:15.155 --> 02:19:18.634
or trees or natural, just something that looks better than what exists

02:19:18.650 --> 02:19:21.393
today. We could probably do that in a budget-neutral way,

02:19:21.473 --> 02:19:25.372
increase the lot size and not have any

02:19:25.420 --> 02:19:28.918
impact. It would beautify our city, increase home values. I see

02:19:29.418 --> 02:19:32.544
that as a, as a positive. But you do—

02:19:32.704 --> 02:19:36.250
I mean, the downside to that, Councilmember Hewish, is that you're going to create

02:19:36.750 --> 02:19:38.496
some maintenance costs for the city. We're going to have to take care of it.

02:19:38.560 --> 02:19:40.503
We're not taking care of it. taking care of it right now, let's be honest.

02:19:41.355 --> 02:19:45.211
So, and I'm not saying that I would be opposed

02:19:45.711 --> 02:19:49.018
to that. I'm just simply saying that there are other consequences of those actions.

02:19:49.629 --> 02:19:52.939
One of the questions that I have is I don't know what it looks like

02:19:53.439 --> 02:19:55.573
is if I, if I take your little orange line and I go to the

02:19:56.073 --> 02:19:58.626
right of the drawing, I don't know what that looks like. And I don't know

02:19:58.642 --> 02:20:02.112
if there are homes there that have encroached into that open space. And if we're

02:20:02.612 --> 02:20:05.004
going to be looking at an application from the homes that come there. Yeah,

02:20:05.069 --> 02:20:08.996
going that way. Yeah. And so my, my concern with that

02:20:09.496 --> 02:20:12.810
specific portion of it is what, what is

02:20:12.842 --> 02:20:15.662
it going to look like? Yeah,

02:20:16.688 --> 02:20:18.995
it doesn't look good. So yeah, so we can, like you said, on the north

02:20:19.495 --> 02:20:22.937
end, I'm, I'm okay with. We can leave— There's not any encroachment in those other

02:20:23.437 --> 02:20:27.392
houses to the park, you know. Oh, they— wait,

02:20:27.648 --> 02:20:30.789
they have a park in that neighborhood? Yeah, that leads to a park.

02:20:31.013 --> 02:20:34.554
That's equal. And a trail. I'm really mad at the city council

02:20:35.054 --> 02:20:37.198
of 2001. We have— yeah,

02:20:37.343 --> 02:20:40.307
that's funny. So, and like I said,

02:20:40.323 --> 02:20:44.073
then you got the area— That doesn't change the fact that that was a developer-required

02:20:44.573 --> 02:20:48.015
open space, and that's why it's there, and it was never improved. That's the answer

02:20:48.515 --> 02:20:51.044
to your question. But the— my point is, is if we don't do something about

02:20:51.544 --> 02:20:55.002
it, it's never going to be improved. So if we're okay

02:20:55.547 --> 02:20:59.569
with open space that collects dirt, sagebrush, brush, all of that stuff,

02:21:00.339 --> 02:21:03.752
and just stays there in between homes when it was promised

02:21:03.800 --> 02:21:07.534
to be something else, then you're right, we should just say, sorry,

02:21:07.566 --> 02:21:11.556
you guys, we're not interested in selling, we want to have a sagebrush tunnel

02:21:11.972 --> 02:21:15.466
in every neighborhood, everyone deserves one, and then move on.

02:21:16.700 --> 02:21:20.081
I'm okay if we have this go through our Community Services Board

02:21:20.581 --> 02:21:23.863
and have them give a recommendation. I think I know probably where I'm going to

02:21:23.911 --> 02:21:28.337
land. And maybe that's our process to institutionalize

02:21:28.369 --> 02:21:31.778
how we deal with these on a case-by-case basis before they come to the council.

02:21:32.249 --> 02:21:35.802
So I think Craig, or Councilmember Whiting, brings up a good point

02:21:36.302 --> 02:21:41.273
to maybe they do have some suggestions and ideas as a process.

02:21:41.709 --> 02:21:45.033
We should probably do that as a first step before it comes to

02:21:49.060 --> 02:21:52.282
If that's the process, then we do it all. Okay.

02:21:52.346 --> 02:21:56.168
So lay out every single neighborhood that has open space between

02:21:56.184 --> 02:21:59.684
the houses with a path. Let's get it all in the same

02:22:00.214 --> 02:22:03.425
conversation, and then let's make a plan. But I'm

02:22:03.925 --> 02:22:07.279
gonna say this also, unless there's this money

02:22:07.343 --> 02:22:10.940
tree that we're not aware of, still has

02:22:10.988 --> 02:22:14.312
to be paid for. Even if you do the most basic minimum things,

02:22:14.745 --> 02:22:18.005
we don't have that money. And so I— that's why

02:22:18.505 --> 02:22:21.850
I'm confused why we keep fighting this fight. What is it that you're

02:22:21.866 --> 02:22:25.624
holding on to? If they want to give

02:22:26.124 --> 02:22:30.577
us money for it, that we could do exactly as Zach suggested and fix

02:22:31.077 --> 02:22:34.290
the rest of the area that's adjacent to that, money that we don't

02:22:34.790 --> 02:22:37.130
have to spend to take care of that piece of land that we don't have

02:22:37.630 --> 02:22:41.124
money to do, why would we be against that? It beautifies the

02:22:41.624 --> 02:22:44.989
area. And it's going to increase the value of those homes,

02:22:45.021 --> 02:22:48.019
by the way. Not just the ones that bought that, but the ones that are

02:22:48.519 --> 02:22:52.051
adjacent to it and all the properties that now benefit from that path being improved.

02:22:52.940 --> 02:22:55.443
Whereas right now, it's not. It's a blight.

02:22:58.095 --> 02:23:01.267
Can I just add one more piece? I mean, I know that we said— we

02:23:01.767 --> 02:23:04.067
say there's not a plan today, but if I was going to start off and

02:23:04.567 --> 02:23:07.335
I was going to try to think of a way to facilitate a plan with

02:23:07.835 --> 02:23:11.910
this, The first check for me would be, is there a regional trail

02:23:12.118 --> 02:23:15.179
or is there a trail connectivity issue in this property that we

02:23:15.679 --> 02:23:18.287
need to address? That should be one of the first checks,

02:23:18.928 --> 02:23:22.101
right? The trail's there, that's a check, it exists.

02:23:23.559 --> 02:23:27.277
But that's the first thing that I would value or evaluate

02:23:27.341 --> 02:23:30.322
if I was looking at the disposal of a property and we were looking to

02:23:30.450 --> 02:23:33.911
improve it, is the connectivity issue and making sure that we

02:23:34.411 --> 02:23:38.210
have connectivity. Then if it's not needed for connectivity,

02:23:38.468 --> 02:23:42.375
then maybe we don't have as big of an appetite to improve

02:23:42.875 --> 02:23:46.109
it or do something else with it. That's— I know we don't have a physical

02:23:46.141 --> 02:23:49.498
plan sitting in front of us today, but that's probably the first

02:23:49.998 --> 02:23:53.177
step in the criteria that I would look at as I evaluate this.

02:23:55.624 --> 02:23:58.561
And I think that's a good thing to make sure that's always a part of

02:23:59.061 --> 02:24:02.778
the LAN, disposal question. We should be looking at whether

02:24:03.278 --> 02:24:04.827
or not it's taking away connectivity,

02:24:06.060 --> 02:24:07.556
trail, that kind of stuff.

02:24:14.543 --> 02:24:17.391
I just want the solution to be fair to all the residents that are there.

02:24:18.502 --> 02:24:21.173
If these residents are given the opportunity to buy a certain amount,

02:24:22.435 --> 02:24:25.457
we should probably be giving that option to a lot of people there.

02:24:26.313 --> 02:24:29.574
I'm also concerned if we give 40 feet here and the last group that came

02:24:29.671 --> 02:24:32.964
we gave 10 or whatever the number is, and those people, we say,

02:24:33.141 --> 02:24:36.243
fine, we'll go back and sell them additional. What if they already put in fences?

02:24:38.280 --> 02:24:41.649
Like, there's cost. Now there's extra cost. In fact, it will cost you guys more

02:24:41.698 --> 02:24:43.326
to fence it than to buy the land.

02:24:45.096 --> 02:24:48.059
And the amount of money that will come in on these land sales is not—

02:24:48.188 --> 02:24:51.119
I'll show you the math. There's not much money.

02:24:51.442 --> 02:24:53.863
There's not— it's not like we're going to get a grundle of money that we

02:24:54.363 --> 02:24:57.375
can go do stuff with. It's not very much money in the big scheme of

02:24:57.875 --> 02:25:01.432
things. So give an alternate plan. How are you going to

02:25:01.932 --> 02:25:04.847
take care of that property? Well, so my plan was this: to have the Community

02:25:05.347 --> 02:25:08.888
Services Board not take a long time, take a few weeks, maybe 8,

02:25:09.963 --> 02:25:13.939
and look at all of that network and make— and help make a recommendation

02:25:13.971 --> 02:25:17.451
to the City Council saying, this is what we think you should do or not

02:25:17.499 --> 02:25:21.448
do. And if they say, Trail is all we need there. Then let's

02:25:21.753 --> 02:25:25.205
then let's figure out how we can start selling this land off

02:25:25.705 --> 02:25:29.396
to those who are adjacent in a fair way, where it's

02:25:29.942 --> 02:25:34.566
equalizing the space as much as possible, so everybody has a pretty similar opportunity.

02:25:35.240 --> 02:25:38.436
And let's get it sold and move on. But we're going to piecemeal it if

02:25:38.936 --> 02:25:42.450
we if we just approve this one now. Then I promise you there's

02:25:42.466 --> 02:25:45.886
going to be additional. Like, requests, and how are we going to judge

02:25:45.998 --> 02:25:49.531
those requests one by one when this is sort of a network in that

02:25:49.595 --> 02:25:53.673
one area? This isn't like a one-off. It's a network of numerous landowners

02:25:54.027 --> 02:25:57.848
that border these corridors. So we have a lot of people that fit into

02:25:57.864 --> 02:26:01.252
this bucket here, not just this row of homes. And I would

02:26:01.752 --> 02:26:05.860
like to see a plan so it's fair. That's all. I'm not saying I'm not

02:26:05.893 --> 02:26:09.441
opposed to selling it. And so I'm not against that idea.

02:26:09.924 --> 02:26:12.629
But I just want a plan that makes sense.

02:26:13.144 --> 02:26:16.654
And then we pull the trigger on that plan. And then

02:26:16.831 --> 02:26:20.150
we're done forever. Because we've been discussing this— at least it's been sitting for decades.

02:26:20.215 --> 02:26:23.753
Let's be the council that figures it out. And this is the solution.

02:26:23.850 --> 02:26:24.399
This is what we're doing.

02:26:33.963 --> 02:26:36.896
Marcus, if we decide that that's what we want to do, do we table this?

02:26:37.804 --> 02:26:40.056
So we can come back and talk about it after we— That would be my

02:26:40.556 --> 02:26:43.805
recommendation. Yeah. Anybody have any—

02:26:43.919 --> 02:26:45.589
anything else they want to say about this?

02:26:48.626 --> 02:26:52.091
Mayor, will you entertain a motion? Yep, go ahead.

02:26:52.709 --> 02:26:55.799
I want to make sure I got the right one here.

02:26:56.254 --> 02:27:00.538
15A. 15A. I move that the city council table

02:27:00.879 --> 02:27:04.407
item 15A, a resolution to be Eagle Mountain City, Utah, authorizing the

02:27:04.907 --> 02:27:08.526
land disposal application to proceed and directing staff to begin the notice

02:27:09.026 --> 02:27:12.337
and public hearing process for a portion of Utah County parcel number 382570108,

02:27:15.720 --> 02:27:19.263
designated as significant real property pursuant to Eagle Mountain Municipal Code

02:27:19.295 --> 02:27:21.357
3.30060.

02:27:21.776 --> 02:27:22.839
Second.

02:27:26.279 --> 02:27:28.365
Okay, I was just going to say, did you want to give direction to the

02:27:28.712 --> 02:27:31.025
To follow Craig's plan or just table it for now and we'll take care of

02:27:31.525 --> 02:27:35.023
that later? I don't— is that— I think we need to move it

02:27:35.055 --> 02:27:38.427
to the Community Service. That's what I'm asking is— The question is, should the 2

02:27:38.927 --> 02:27:42.682
be tied together? That's what we— Marcus, I'm asking you, should we— do

02:27:43.182 --> 02:27:46.856
we need to give an instruction to have the Community Service Board review all open

02:27:47.356 --> 02:27:51.288
space between houses as part of this application? If that's what you want

02:27:51.788 --> 02:27:55.505
to do, you can do that, yeah, sure. So moved. So seconded.

02:27:56.245 --> 02:28:00.026
Okay, but the open space just in this, this particular area—

02:28:00.106 --> 02:28:03.516
I was clear. I said anytime that it goes between houses, let's have them evaluate

02:28:04.016 --> 02:28:06.332
it and give us a plan. Okay,

02:28:07.490 --> 02:28:11.028
good. All right, we have a motion then by Councilmember Wood and a second

02:28:11.528 --> 02:28:14.845
by Councilmember Whiting, and we'll start the vote with Councilmember Hewish. Yes.

02:28:15.136 --> 02:28:15.864
Councilmember Clark.

02:28:18.305 --> 02:28:21.194
Sorry, yes. Councilmember Wright? Yes.

02:28:21.436 --> 02:28:24.021
Councilmember Whiting? Yes. Councilmember Wood? Yes.

02:28:24.602 --> 02:28:28.123
Okay, that passes. Thank you. Mayor, can I, can I just

02:28:28.623 --> 02:28:33.601
ask a question and maybe make a suggestion? Sure. Can we— could

02:28:33.617 --> 02:28:37.095
we consider adopting or changing our code for the land

02:28:37.595 --> 02:28:40.631
disposal applications to include a recommendation from our

02:28:41.131 --> 02:28:44.522
Community Services Board so that before we ever see it, that comes to

02:28:45.022 --> 02:28:48.813
us with the application? Sure. That will clean up the process.

02:28:48.877 --> 02:28:51.345
We're not going to be telling you we're going to table it, we're going to

02:28:51.845 --> 02:28:55.207
get back to it. It will be part of the process to initiate it to

02:28:55.707 --> 02:28:59.101
come to us. So I will say this, that while the majority of our land

02:28:59.213 --> 02:29:02.899
disposal applications come for this type of situation

02:29:03.399 --> 02:29:06.553
where it's open space, that's not 100% of them. And in some cases,

02:29:06.665 --> 02:29:10.655
it wouldn't make sense to have a recommendation from the Community Service

02:29:10.735 --> 02:29:14.652
Board. To use an example, the Kroger development

02:29:15.230 --> 02:29:18.474
where we sold a small piece of land that was not open space,

02:29:18.522 --> 02:29:22.456
was other land that the city owned to Smith's Food and

02:29:22.956 --> 02:29:26.165
Drug. And maybe it's just that contain a trail or that part of a trail

02:29:26.665 --> 02:29:29.312
network. If you just look at that, Marcus, and help us clean that up so

02:29:29.812 --> 02:29:33.101
that when we get the information— If it's existing open space, I think that it

02:29:33.601 --> 02:29:37.110
makes sense. Okay, thank you. So you're

02:29:37.610 --> 02:29:40.310
probably looking like, now what? Say that again. Are you looking at us like,

02:29:40.810 --> 02:29:42.890
now what does that mean? Yeah, now what does that mean? Like, so what can

02:29:43.390 --> 02:29:45.890
I do to help? Basically, we're going to take this back. We're going to let

02:29:46.390 --> 02:29:49.430
our Community Services Board go through all the land that's like this. Most of it's

02:29:49.930 --> 02:29:53.310
going to be in this area and give a recommendation of how that land

02:29:53.810 --> 02:29:56.230
should be used. Then they're going to come back and give us a recommendation,

02:29:56.530 --> 02:29:59.650
and we're going to make a decision on your application. Got it.

02:30:00.150 --> 02:30:03.191
And I can get you in touch with the chair of that board. Okay.

02:30:03.272 --> 02:30:06.692
So you can have open communication. Any future, like, plans that we would

02:30:07.192 --> 02:30:09.839
have, because, like, if we had more, I would build a detached garage so I

02:30:10.339 --> 02:30:14.624
can actually park my cars in a garage. Now you're asking

02:30:14.640 --> 02:30:18.188
for too much. Okay. My garage is— No, you were singing the

02:30:18.688 --> 02:30:22.074
song of our people. One thing at a time. Keep going. I can't use— You'll

02:30:22.122 --> 02:30:25.189
have to follow the code, whatever, you know, if this all comes to

02:30:25.237 --> 02:30:28.623
fruition. So yeah, we'll start with the first step. Yeah, I really

02:30:29.123 --> 02:30:32.982
appreciate your guys' time. So really, just for all

02:30:33.094 --> 02:30:36.539
you guys' information who own property there, Zach is our

02:30:37.039 --> 02:30:40.626
liaison for the Community Services Board, as well as Melissa. You can stay

02:30:41.126 --> 02:30:44.071
in touch with them directly or through Lacey. She handles— I've been in contact with

02:30:44.571 --> 02:30:48.189
her. So she'll keep you posted. It's gonna be— do they meet once a month

02:30:48.689 --> 02:30:51.859
or twice a month? Once. So I mean, this is— it's gonna take

02:30:52.359 --> 02:30:54.987
a couple of months. Is that in I mean, it could go on the next

02:30:55.487 --> 02:30:58.584
agenda. It could go on the next agenda, but I mean, they've got a lot

02:30:59.084 --> 02:31:02.405
of things they're already working on, so time— There's also a map that was

02:31:02.905 --> 02:31:06.066
started when Todd was still here. I was gonna— I was

02:31:06.566 --> 02:31:10.562
just asking Brad about that. So like, I think there's a really good jumping-off point.

02:31:10.626 --> 02:31:14.464
It's not like they're gonna have to comb through the parcel map to find them.

02:31:14.977 --> 02:31:18.670
I think they're already probably highlighted, and they're just gonna have to make those determinations.

02:31:19.056 --> 02:31:22.566
If we could get them the map and the addresses, they could

02:31:22.582 --> 02:31:26.350
physically go see them on their own before they come to meet, and that

02:31:26.850 --> 02:31:29.941
would help them understand the area as well. One of the other things

02:31:29.973 --> 02:31:33.483
as far as process that on this particular item at a

02:31:33.983 --> 02:31:37.812
broad scale, when Todd was making that map, it was the council had the

02:31:38.312 --> 02:31:41.515
opportunity to designate significant versus insignificant lands,

02:31:41.980 --> 02:31:44.977
which would have a different process and, and make some of them easier.

02:31:45.346 --> 02:31:47.851
So if we knew there were areas like this in the city that you wanted

02:31:48.351 --> 02:31:51.689
to designate as insignificant, then the process would

02:31:52.189 --> 02:31:55.719
be much cleaner. And it's just— It's much simpler.

02:31:56.120 --> 02:31:57.678
It doesn't have the public hearing.

02:31:57.983 --> 02:32:01.869
It's processed administratively.

02:32:02.286 --> 02:32:06.188
And so that's kind of where that was headed with the map. And if that's

02:32:06.688 --> 02:32:09.720
something you want to do, when we were able to identify that if it's all

02:32:09.785 --> 02:32:13.810
these areas and it's 10 feet or 20 or all of it, then just

02:32:13.938 --> 02:32:18.122
designate it insignificant. And then if people want it, it's easy. And that

02:32:19.212 --> 02:32:22.595
changes how that works. My understanding is when we incorporated

02:32:23.095 --> 02:32:25.961
this board, that was the primary goal of this board,

02:32:26.026 --> 02:32:29.793
was to go take our open space, make sense of what is usable,

02:32:29.873 --> 02:32:32.214
unusable, and make recommendations to us.

02:32:33.159 --> 02:32:36.838
And I would like us to refocus back on

02:32:36.871 --> 02:32:39.551
that if we can, because I'm going to tell you, all of the other things

02:32:40.051 --> 02:32:41.955
are great. They're not as pressing as that is,

02:32:43.184 --> 02:32:47.127
in my opinion. All right.

02:32:47.224 --> 02:32:47.722
Appreciate it.

02:32:50.550 --> 02:32:53.874
We will move on to the next item, 16A, which is a public hearing.

02:32:55.303 --> 02:33:00.342
This is an ordinance of Eagle Mountain City amending the Municipal Code Section 1625.040

02:33:00.519 --> 02:33:03.964
and 1710.030 definitions

02:33:04.464 --> 02:33:07.539
and 1760.040 60-040, General Landscape Provisions.

02:33:08.411 --> 02:33:10.848
And Miss Fuchs.

02:33:20.392 --> 02:33:23.807
Good evening, Mayor and Council. This one's pretty straightforward.

02:33:23.856 --> 02:33:27.174
We've talked about it a few times before. It has kind of

02:33:27.674 --> 02:33:30.918
2 parts. One part is we need to make some changes in

02:33:31.017 --> 02:33:34.543
our code due to changes made by the state legislature.

02:33:35.233 --> 02:33:38.937
And we also discussed if we wanted to do anything else with our

02:33:39.437 --> 02:33:43.090
code in-house. And so it's also adopting some

02:33:43.590 --> 02:33:47.371
changes requested by the council. So the first one with the state

02:33:47.419 --> 02:33:50.738
is that we need to make it so that we allow

02:33:51.027 --> 02:33:54.282
residents to remove landscaping if the purpose

02:33:54.410 --> 02:33:58.226
is to be able to create defensible space within a WUI

02:33:58.354 --> 02:34:02.073
zone. So that's the wildland urban interface for the benefit

02:34:02.573 --> 02:34:05.767
of the public. Those are areas that have higher wildfire dangers.

02:34:07.997 --> 02:34:11.837
And so this— what this does is we're also— we

02:34:12.337 --> 02:34:15.178
talked about the count to the council about if we wanted to do something as

02:34:15.226 --> 02:34:18.718
a city, and there were some recommendations by the fire officials, but there was

02:34:18.750 --> 02:34:22.171
some hesitancy to do something citywide and wanted to look at it

02:34:22.203 --> 02:34:25.833
more on a case-by-case basis. So what this does is we'll

02:34:25.994 --> 02:34:29.400
add a notation to final plat that the land is within

02:34:29.448 --> 02:34:33.029
the WUI to help the residents be able to identify that. We're going

02:34:33.045 --> 02:34:36.304
to make those state code changes. And then also

02:34:36.497 --> 02:34:40.094
we've made some internal process changes to let the council

02:34:40.126 --> 02:34:43.867
